{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed807260302/13/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"60d73be6\" lines=\"22-39\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse colored marker pattern by reflecting it across a full-row/full-column cross.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background and one non-background cross color that\nfills exactly one complete row and one complete column. All other foreground\npixels are sparse markers away from that cross.\n\nRule\nKeep the input unchanged, and for each marker pixel copy its color to the\npositions obtained by reflecting it across the cross row, across the cross\ncolumn, and across both axes. Reflected positions outside the grid are\nclipped.\n\nKey insight\nThe full cross is not a copied object; it is the pair of mirror axes for\ncompleting the quadrant pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"edcc2ff0\" lines=\"47-63\">\n<description>\nKeep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.\n\nInput structure\nA black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers\nin the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has\na unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.\n\nRule\nIn the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground\nobject to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0\nwhose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.\n\nKey insight\nThe top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects\nsurvive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e376de54\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nAlign parallel colored line segments so they all share the same start and end positions as a reference line.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several parallel line segments (diagonal, horizontal, or vertical), each a different color, running in the same direction but starting/ending at different positions.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the dominant direction shared by all segments.\n- Pick the median segment (by perpendicular offset) as the reference.\n- Shift every segment along the shared direction so its start aligns with the reference's start, then trim or extend it to match the reference's length.\n- Grid size stays the same; isolated single pixels are untouched.\n\nKey insight: The reference is the median line, not the longest; all segments are snapped to its extent regardless of their original length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c61be7dc\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nFlatten the interior object into a centered line and move the guide bars inward to bracket it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- One non-background guide color touches the boundary and forms exactly two full parallel bars plus a split perpendicular center bar.\n- The other non-background color is a single 8-connected interior object whose area determines the flattened line length.\n\nRule:\n- If the guide pair is vertical, make a vertical object-colored line centered between those bars; otherwise use the analogous horizontal line.\n- The line length equals the number of interior object pixels and is centered on the split perpendicular guide bar.\n- Rebuild the guide color as the perpendicular center bar plus two full bars immediately beside the flattened object line.\n\nKey insight:\n- The guide bars supply only the orientation and center; the interior object's pixel count supplies the length of the final one-cell-thick line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"470c91de\" lines=\"48-65\">\n<description>\nMove each teal-cornered filled rectangle one cell diagonally toward its teal corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- Dominant background surrounds several filled rectangular colored objects.\n- Each object has exactly one teal control pixel replacing one corner cell of\n  its rectangle.\n\nRule:\n- Treat the teal pixel as the missing corner of its colored rectangle.\n- Erase each original rectangle footprint, including the teal pixel.\n- Repaint the completed rectangle shifted one cell diagonally in the direction\n  of that teal corner.\n\nKey insight:\n- The control pixel's corner position gives both the missing cell to restore\n  and the diagonal motion vector.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2faf500b\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nRemove each fracture marker line and push the surrounding object pixels one cell away from it.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid with 8-connected foreground objects made from a body color and a rarer marker color.\n- Each object has a non-square bbox; the marker pixels form one complete fracture line across the shorter bbox axis.\n\nRule\n- Erase all marker pixels.\n- For a wide object, each row has one marker; body pixels left of it shift one cell left and body pixels right of it shift one cell right.\n- For a tall object, each column has one marker; body pixels above it shift one cell up and body pixels below it shift one cell down.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker line is a crack through the object, so the two sides separate by one pixel perpendicular to the crack.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5a719d11\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nWithin each horizontal pair of two-color panels, swap the motif masks and recolor each pasted mask with the source panel's base color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A separator color forms full blank rows and at least one full blank column.\n- The non-separator area is split into row bands, each containing exactly two same-shaped rectangular panels.\n- Each panel has a dominant base color and one minority motif color.\n\nRule:\n- For every row band, clear each panel to its own base color.\n- Paste the right panel's motif mask into the left panel using the right base color.\n- Paste the left panel's motif mask into the right panel using the left base color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The motif colors themselves are distractors; only each motif's shape mask and the two panel base colors are exchanged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e84fef15\" lines=\"39-59\">\n<description>\nReconstruct the repeated tile from its separated copies and mark the missing pixels in the damaged copy blue.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a lattice of equal-size blocks separated by one-cell solid rows\n  and columns of the same separator color.\n- All blocks are copies of one motif except exactly one block, where some\n  motif pixels have been overwritten by the motif background color.\n- Blue is absent from the input and is reserved as the output marker color.\n\nRule:\n- Recover the motif by taking the unique majority color at each local block\n  coordinate across all block copies.\n- Find the single block that differs from this consensus motif.\n- Return the consensus motif, recoloring the differing local coordinates blue.\n\nKey insight:\n- The repeated copies provide redundancy: the damaged positions are exactly\n  the coordinates where the lone nonconforming copy has background instead of\n  the consensus motif color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8e301a54\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nMove every foreground object straight downward by a number of rows equal to its own pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- The foreground is made of separated 4-connected monochromatic objects.\n- Objects may move partly below the bottom edge of the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\n- For each foreground object, compute its area in pixels.\n- Redraw the object at the same columns and that many rows lower, clipping pixels below the grid.\n\nKey insight:\n- The downward displacement is the object's own area; color and shape only identify which pixels move together.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"21897d95\" lines=\"283-301\">\n<description>\nRepaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions.\n- Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries.\n- Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color.\n- An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.\n\nRule:\n- For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint.\n- The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color.\n- For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement.\n- All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
