1-cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/5/code.py:64:#   a 4-neighbor shell around its exposed edges; this guarantees a closed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/1/code.py:29:#    - D10's single-variant used a 4-neighbor shell around an irregular cavity. Under 4-connected tracing that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/1/code.py:30:#      shell often breaks into multiple pieces, so it cannot be reused directly. The combo instead uses the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/1/code.py:31:#      cavity's 8-neighbor shell as the frame ring; this keeps one enclosed cavity while making the ring itself\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/1/code.py:334:    Build a 4-connected irregular frame as the cavity's 8-neighbor shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/1/code.py:336:    Using the Moore shell rather than the 4-neighbor shell is the key combo adjustment:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/15/code.py:121:    - In this combo, those loops are 45-degree diamond-flavored shells whose enclosed bg regions are irregular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/15/code.py:132:      cavity with a single-color shell attached to exactly one seed's drawing. The solver can stay shape-agnostic as long\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/12/code.py:28:#   the output can simultaneously show a grey source-position shell and a docked\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/12/code.py:67:#   moved objects leave a grey hollow shell behind, and only fully reaching objects\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:29:#   combo: a clean green object with shell insets [0, 2, 4, 6] has value 4 directly,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:32:#   visible shell depth; the padding source changes from a separate noise color to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:37:#   object varies in depth and overall side length while every shell stays visually\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:48:#   components. That assumption is now false; the value is encoded directly by shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:63:# - A human looking at examples can directly count nested shells and see that output bar\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:70:#   shells are separated by black space both within each object and between objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:74:#   \"same object color -> same output row color, row length = shell depth\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:79:# - Samples should exhibit nested-shell depth as the dominant signal: all non-black\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:81:#   gap, and no non-black pixel may appear outside the constructed shell masks.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:87:# - Within that bbox, every true shell is a complete 1px square border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:181:def _detect_shell_insets(mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[list[int], np.ndarray]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:213:    insets, _cropped = _detect_shell_insets(g == int(color))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:231:    - There is no extra noise color and no inter-object occlusion, so every shell is fully visible.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:337:    for idx, (color, _layers, shell_arr) in enumerate(frames):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:339:        rs, cs = np.where(shell_arr)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:376:        shell_mask = _build_nested_frame(int(layers), build_max_side)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:377:        if shell_mask is None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/21/code.py:379:        frames.append((int(color), int(layers), shell_mask))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:28:#   combo: three colors with shell depths [1, 3, 4] are still ordered [1, 3, 4], but\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:30:#   difference 1: the counted thing changes from \"enclosed rooms\" to \"nested shells\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:39:#   without making the grid so crowded that square shells become hard to parse.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:49:#   on those overlap cells. That assumption still works if the reconstructed shell masks\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:57:# - Overlaps are allowed only on non-protected shell pixels and only in a small fraction\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:58:#   of each shell set, otherwise shell detection or human readability degrades.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:70:# - The scattered pad color is restricted to non-shell cells. This keeps the shells thin\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:72:# - Protected corners and side midpoints are preserved on each shell so the nested-square\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:86:# - Every real object's visible bbox remains square and contains all shell corners.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:87:# - Each detected shell keeps enough pixels on all four sides to exceed the hit threshold.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:88:# - The pad color never forms a square shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:89:# - When a tie exists, the tied pair overlaps on shell pixels so `_cover_order()` has\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/89565ca0/5/code.py:151:def _detect_shell_insets(mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[list[int], np.ndarray]:\n/home/