ll] = int(OBJ)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/32/code.py:211:    stamped with one special marker color, and all disconnected sprites survive only as hollow shells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2c181942/20/code.py:78:# - the shell mask is flipud-symmetric\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2c181942/20/code.py:250:    # Symmetry stays on the shell silhouette, while the inner accent acts as a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/34/code.py:43:#     sliding. This remains valid because the generator still uses closed shells for emitters.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/20270e3b/23/code.py:550:    shell = _dilate4(_mask_from_points(socket, (H, W))) & allowed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/20270e3b/23/code.py:551:    if not shell.any():\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/20270e3b/23/code.py:554:    body = np.array(shell, dtype=bool)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/13/code.py:26:#        * Closed shell `000 / 0.0 / 000` flush to the separator still triggers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/13/code.py:27:#        * Open `C-left` shell `000 / ..0 / 000` used to fail because the bg region leaks\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/13/code.py:30:#        * Right-open `C-right` shell `000 / 0.. / 000` still does not trigger because its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/13/code.py:56:#    - Open emitters (`C-left`, `L`) are visibly different from closed shells in the left pane,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/15/code.py:52:#    - Frames remain 4x4 shells around 2x2 cores, with the original non-overlap spacing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/5/code.py:47:#       making the deeper BR/BL windows so coarse that the nested structure disappears.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/46/code.py:53:#    Frames keep the original 4x4 shell around a 2x2 monochrome block and obey\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:51:#     \"break shell, then break shell again\" pattern.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:73:#     output, giving the human solver evidence that only specific shell breaks happen.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:149:    def add_route(name, orientation, opening, shell1, gate2, shell2, gate3, distractors):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:156:                \"shell1\": shell1,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:158:                \"shell2\": shell2,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:318:        shell1_r, shell1_c = route[\"shell1\"]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:321:        if int(grid[shell1_r, shell1_c]) != MAROON:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:410:    Key insight: Reachability is no longer static. The nested frames define a staged escape path, and the visible wall removals prove that later maroons are not initially reachable; they become collectible only because earlier BFS layers crack open the next shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:453:    banned = {route[\"opening\"], route[\"shell1\"], route[\"shell2\"], *route[\"gate2\"], *route[\"gate3\"]}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/53/code.py:485:    sources = [route[\"shell1\"], route[\"shell2\"], *_choose_outer_sources(route)]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db0c5428/32/code.py:62:#    the input, and the copied directional blocks do not stop at the 5x5 shell. The\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db0c5428/32/code.py:63:#    second shell repeats the same corner/edge identities one more step outward, which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:37:#       setup: 10 reachable maroons on the same bfs shell around the destination\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:38:#       original: 9 chosen maroons are the row-major earliest shell cells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:39:#       combo: 9 chosen maroons are the clockwise-earliest shell cells from the top\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:63:#       - angle-visible boards must guarantee a same-distance shell with at least 10 maroons and different row-vs-angle top-9 subsets\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:68:#     - all shell searches are computed from the same bfs metric used by the solver, so generation never depends on hidden geometry\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:71:#     - the clockwise shell prefix outside and the clockwise spiral prefix inside both reuse the same center, so the rotational cue is coherent rather than cluttered\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:429:    shells: dict[int, list[tuple[int, int]]] = {}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:433:            shells.setdefault(d, []).append((r, c))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:435:    shell_items = list(shells.items())\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:436:    random.shuffle(shell_items)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/15/code.py:437:    for _, cells in shell_items:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db0c5428/44/code.py:300:      stripe interpolation rather than a single sparse reflection shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/33/code.py:61:#    nested structure readable instead of cluttered.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:28:#      diagonal rays from selected outer corners, and the object has a 1-cell shell whose informative\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:29:#      pixels are shell-corner markers. After alignment the whole shell is removed.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:32:#         combo object:      shell corners TL=red, BR=blue, core size 7x7\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:49:#      Replaced by `_guide_corners()`, which reads only shell corners.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:51:#      full shell whose semantics are \"orientation carrier\"; after matching, the solver removes the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:52:#      whole 1-cell shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:55:#      2. The center object is one interior component with a 1-cell shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:56:#      3. Only shell corners differ from the shell fill.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:63:#    - The shell is nonzero everywhere, so the whole object remains a single 4-connected component.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:66:#      on the shell corners of the central square object. Across examples, the output is always the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:70:#      shell. No region mixes multiple semantics on the same cells, so cues remain separable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:73:#      diagonal and on one shell corner, so the color correspondence is structurally signposted.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:147:    Align a shell-framed square core to diagonal corner controls via dihedral transforms.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:151:    - A central square object: asymmetric square core + 1-cell shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:152:    - Exactly three shell corners are recolored as guide markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/11/code.py:156:    - Remove the entire 1-cell shell and output the square core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:39:#      3. The object becomes `core + full 1px shell`. Only two opposite shell corners are recolored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:40:#         The shell corner sitting on the chosen quadrant gets the row-half color of that quadrant\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:41:#         (`up` or `down`); the opposite shell corner gets the column-half color (`left` or `right`).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:45:#      aligned object shell markers = {tr:2, bl:7}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:50:#      and finally removes the full shell to output the core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:57:#    - The shell always has exactly two opposite colored corners, keeping the cue simple enough\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:66:#      only two opposite shell corners differ from the fill.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:68:#      carrier is the entire shell, so the correct crop is always `t[1:-1,1:-1]`.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:73:#      4. the shell markers occupy exactly two opposite corners.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:83:#      colors, then remove the shell\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:86:#      and the two shell-corner markers are isolated. This avoids layered clutter.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:231:    Align a shell-framed rectangle to one quadrant of an internal colored cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:235:    - One interior rectangle with a full 1px shell. Exactly two opposite shell corners are recolored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:239:    - Rotate/mirror the rectangle until its diagonal shell-corner colors match one quadrant's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/fc7cae8d/7/code.py:241:    - Remove the whole shell and output the core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/25/code.py:79:    \"keep shell, color interior\" rather than \"recolor body\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/25/code.py:84:  - The output in any local area has only background, a grey shell, and one fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/25/code.py:278:    - If the sprite's concavity count appears in the legend, keep the grey shell and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/15/code.py:56:#    - Tie breaking inside one distance shell is top-to-bottom then left-to-right. The generator\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/15/code.py:84:#    - The key waters occupy two distinct wall-distance shells: two pixels at distance 1 and one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/15/code.py:336:      top-to-bottom, left-to-right tie break inside one distance shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:31:#   then kept extending with the fixed 2/1 alternation. This made outer shells grow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:38:#   colored shell consists of two consecutive major runs (typically one horizontal and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:39:#   one vertical leg), so shell 1 carries the 1+1 pair, shell 2 carries the 2+3 pair,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:40:#   shell 3 carries the 5+8 pair, etc. This makes the non-uniform growth directly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:43:#   then starts shell 1 by turning into the spiral and realizing straight lengths 1,1.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:44:#   The next shell uses color 2 while the straight lengths become 2 then 3. The third\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:45:#   shell uses color 3 while lengths become 5 then 8. Compared with the base task, the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:51:#   into just a few tiny shells and becomes visually indistinguishable from a generic\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:54:#   prefix on all four walkers and at least 5 visible color shells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:59:#   boolean wall mask, because output colors now vary by shell. `occ` stores geometry;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:63:#   can destroy valid colored shells, so it is removed entirely.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:66:# - The solver also assumes the desired shell count fits ARC colors. The accepted\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:82:#   markers, legends, or overlays. Colors are reserved exclusively for shell identity.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:83:# - The 1-cell orthogonal gap rule is preserved, so adjacent shells remain separable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:86:# - Colors increase monotonically from center to outer shells. The mapping is structural:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:87:#   shell index -> color index. The solver never uses hidden IDs or non-visible ordering\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:178:    def shell_color(seg_idx: int) -> int:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:220:        color = shell_color(agent[\"seg_idx\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:271:    Grow four clockwise spiral walls from a hollow teal cross, but let shell growth follow Fibonacci-sized straight runs and color shells outward from 1 upward.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:282:    - Every visible shell shares one color: shell 1 is color 1, shell 2 is color 2, shell 3 is color 3, and so on.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/26/code.py:284:    Key insight: The task is no longer \"uniform spiral then recolor by radius\". The color boundaries are tied to the same construction process that uses Fibonacci straight-run lengths, so the widening shell bands reveal the recurrence.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/27/code.py:9:#    same outer puzzle shell, but replaces the inner packing rule with an explicit sequence:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/24/code.py:139:    - One visible quantity in the input drives two geometric effects at once. Arm length first pushes the launch point farther away from the cross, then also sets how thick the allowed spiral shell is, so the output becomes \"inner spokes + outer finite rings\" instead of a border-filling maze.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/32/code.py:31:#      does not degenerate into full recoloring or into dots floating in tiny shells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/32/code.py:150:    Key insight: Once the spiral is discretized into its square envelopes, the whole task reduces to reading the cross radius and painting evenly spaced rectangular shells while protecting the source cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/38/code.py:23:#      large nested structure where every visible chamber contains a same-color island with another hole inside.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/28/code.py:160:    # Outer inverted shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/28/code.py:173:    # Inner inverted basin nested inside the outer shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/28/code.py:226:    - Other colors draw a large top-anchored outer shell and a smaller inverted basin nested inside it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/2/code.py:28:#      colors just the single interior hole pink while leaving the shell grey.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/2/code.py:41:#      the interior cavities, not the shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/2/code.py:56:#      shell, and one legend color inside the shell. Template spacing and bbox\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/2/code.py:175:    - If a match exists, keep the grey shell unchanged and paint each enclosed hole\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/44/code.py:3:# Name: 矩形环框架+同心环内部结构\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/44/code.py:9:#     shell, but the visible structure changes in two coupled ways.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/10/code.py:3:# Name: 填入框架洞中+矩形环框架\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/10/code.py:18:#    - 本融合版输入里直接给出一个已经摆正的完整矩形环；它四边闭合、内部留空，其他碎片都散落在环外。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/10/code.py:29:#    - 新 solver 引入的新前提是“存在唯一完整矩形环，且其 bbox 内除了环色就是背景”；生成器会严格构造并校验这一点。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/10/code.py:34:#    - 输入最显眼的对象总是一块“矩形环带洞”，输出则是同一矩形被内部补满，框架位置不变，因果关系可以直接肉眼跟踪。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/10/code.py:41:#    - 规则可概括为: “先认出完整矩形环，再把外部碎片拼回环内部”，不需要借助任何不可见元数据。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/10/code.py:43:#    - 假设唯一完整矩形环存在: 生成器只放一块周边闭合的单色大环，面积也远大于任一小块。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/10/code.py:48:#      `hole_area == outside_fill_area > 0`，且 bbox 内非背景只出现在完整矩形环周边；这正是“封闭环 + 洞填充”的核心信号。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/da515329/5/code.py:216:      old layer-by-layer growth, so the visual switches from dense spiral shells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/_logs/final_summary.json:114:    \"name\": \"填入框架洞中+矩形环框架\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/_logs/final_summary.json:522:    \"name\": \"矩形环框架+同心环内部结构\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/_logs/parent_test_results.json:155:    \"name\": \"填入框架洞中+矩形环框架\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e8686506/_logs/parent_test_results.json:713:    \"name\": \"矩形环框架+同心环内部结构\",\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/31/code.py:26:#      8-neighborhood. On a hollow object with a thin shell and one missing outer corner, the old\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/edb79dae/D5/3/code.py:88:    \"\"\"Keep only the 4-neighborhood outer shell of a binary sprite mask.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:30:#     - small-hole objects: shell teal (8), hole magenta (6), outline green (3)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:31:#     - large-hole objects: shell green (3), hole yellow (4), outline blue (1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:34:#     - original logic: a 1x3 hole and a 2x2 hole both become shell=8, hole=6, outline=2\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:36:#     - fused logic step 2: area 3 -> shell=8 and hole=6, then hole color 6 implies complement outline 3\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:37:#     - fused logic step 3: area 4 -> shell=3 and hole=4, then hole color 4 implies complement outline 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:44:#   - although yellow holes match the background color, the surrounding green shell makes the enclosed area still readable\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:61:#   - the two hollow classes differ in both shell color and outline color, so the change from the original task is obvious\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:62:#   - the chained rule is inferable because the same hole-area split that changes shell/hole recolor also predicts the outline color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:153:    - For hollow components, first sum the enclosed hole area: total area <= 3 gives teal shell (8) with magenta holes (6),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:154:      while total area > 3 gives green shell (3) with yellow holes (4).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:159:    - The transform is chained: detect holes, measure their total area, recolor shell and hole, then infer the outline color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:175:            shell_color, hole_color, outline_color = _hole_style(total_hole_area)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:176:            out[mask] = shell_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:459:        shell_color, hole_color, outline_color = _hole_style(total_hole_area)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/33/code.py:464:        if set(map(int, out[mask])) != {shell_color}:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/1/code.py:69:#     - Hollow objects advertise their class directly through teal shell + magenta cavity, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/3/code.py:24:#        original task: a shell with two holes would be recolored uniformly as teal body with\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/3/code.py:26:#        combo task: the same shell is first recognized as a 2-hole object, so its body becomes\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/3/code.py:31:#      pick the shell color and measure each hole to pick its fill color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/3/code.py:58:#      and from neighboring holes. This guarantees enclosed holes and a 4-connected shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/3/code.py:184:    count the number of enclosed holes to recolor the shell, then measure every hole's area to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:19:#     - every top-level object still has a blue (1) outer shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:20:#     - exactly 2 objects are nested in the generator: each shell encloses one rectangular pocket,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:28:#         area <= 3  -> blue shell becomes teal (8), inner object becomes magenta (6)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:29:#         area > 3   -> blue shell becomes green (3), inner object becomes yellow (4)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:30:#     - the outline is computed on the occupied union of shell + inner object, so no red ring is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:31:#       drawn inside the shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:33:#     - original task: a blue shell around a 2x2 background hole becomes teal shell + magenta hole\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:34:#     - D3-only variant: the same shell around a colored 2x2 inner object becomes teal shell while\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:36:#     - combo here: the same shell around a colored 2x2 inner object has enclosed area 4, so it\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:37:#       becomes green shell + yellow inner object; a 1x3 inner object instead becomes teal + magenta\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:48:#   - `find_components(... colors=[FG_COLOR])` assumes every transformable outer shell is blue; the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:50:#   - `_interior_regions` uses shell geometry only; this stays valid because nested objects are fully\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:51:#     contained inside pockets carved from the blue shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:60:#   - placement uses the occupied union of shell and inner object, not just the blue shell, so nested\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:62:#   - nested shells are produced by carving a single interior pocket from a connected shell mask, then\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:68:#     a larger one and infer that area, not identity, drives the shell/inner recolor pair\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:69:#   - even though the large branch turns the inner object yellow like the background, the shell turns\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:73:#   - there is at most one pocket per nested shell and exactly one inner color in the input, avoiding\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:74:#     clutter or ambiguous multi-object interactions inside the shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:84:#     area changes both shell and inner recolor outcomes\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:167:    Add red outer outlines around blue shells, recoloring nested objects by enclosed area.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:170:    - A mostly uniform background contains several blue 4-connected outer shells, plus some solid blue objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:171:    - Some blue shells enclose a fully internal pocket occupied by a smaller non-background inner object instead of background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:175:    - For a blue shell that encloses other colored pixels, measure the total enclosed non-background area.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:176:    - Area <= 3 gives teal shell (8) with magenta inner object (6); area > 3 gives green shell (3) with yellow inner object (4).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:178:    Key insight: The shell still defines the region to inspect, but the decisive signal is no longer\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:179:    an empty hole. You must detect a nested occupied interior, treat shell + inner object as one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:194:            shell_color, inner_color = _nested_colors(total_inner_area)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:195:            out[outer_mask] = shell_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:365:def _make_nested_shell(target_area: int) -> np.ndarray | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/7/code.py:428:        outer = _make_nested_shell(target_area)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:27:#   - 原题：红/绿只是调色板中的两个槽位，整块区域仍按边界深度涂成矩形环带。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D1/2/code.py:29:#     中间出现一条楼梯状分界线，而不是围绕房间边界的矩形环。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/5/code.py:56:#      colored shells are stacked.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:30:#       - each source color also has its own hollow-shell recolor target\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:32:#         source->outline and source->hollow-shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:37:#       - every hollow shell became teal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:49:#       for the outline map and showing that shell recoloring triggers only for hollow objects\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:66:#     - two hollow objects per sample show at least two source->shell mappings directly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:74:#     - output structure stays readable: shell color on hollow bodies, magenta in holes, and a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:79:#         1. D3-only style: source-specific hollow shell, but all outlines forced red\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:80:#         2. D12-only style: source-specific outlines, but all hollow shells forced teal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:84:#     - at least one hollow object must use a non-blue source color, otherwise the shell-map\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:190:    - Hollow objects recolor their shell by another fixed source-color mapping, fill enclosed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/23/code.py:195:    objects, and source->hollow-shell for the subset that actually contains holes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:18:#      constructed as hollow shells around a rectangular hole.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:24:#      This means the blue shell has been eroded before we ask whether it still encloses anything.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:26:#      original order on a hollow shell with padding (top,left,right,bottom) = (1,2,2,2):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:27:#      detect hole first -> whole shell becomes teal, hole becomes magenta, then the outer teal edge\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:29:#      fused order -> outer boundary turns red first, so the one-cell top shell disappears from the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:32:#    - A second hollow shell with all paddings >= 2 behaves differently: after the red shrink, the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:48:#    - The original hollow recolor covered the full shell before outlining. Here that would erase the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:68:#      on the already-shrunk blue residue rather than the original blue shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/32/code.py:234:    - Some of those blue components are hollow shells with enclosed rectangular cavities.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/22/code.py:60:#    - Hollow objects advertise their class through teal shell + magenta cavity, providing\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/17/code.py:36:# - 若空心对象只是普通矩形环，洞 8 邻域根本碰不到外轮廓，D9/2 会退化成原题。因此至少\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:17:#      objects change inside the object itself (shell -> teal, hole -> magenta).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:19:#      object, the old solver would have produced a teal shell with a red outline if such an input\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:22:#      gets an orange outline. The source color controls hollow-shell recoloring, while hole-status\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:27:#      D3-only: blue ring -> teal shell + magenta hole + red outline; green L stays green + red outline.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:28:#      fused: blue ring -> teal shell + magenta hole + red outline; green L stays green + orange outline.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:30:#    - Use exactly three input colors and exactly three hollow-shell targets, keeping the mapping\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:34:#    - Force one source color to appear once as hollow and once as solid, proving the shell recolor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:38:#    - The original `out[mask] = 8` no longer holds; hollow shell recoloring must depend on the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:53:#      shell targets, making both inference layers visible in a single example.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:55:#    - No extra markers are added beyond shell recolor, hole fill, and one-pixel outlines.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:57:#      borders, and hollow shells chosen from teal/gray/maroon.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:129:    - If an object has enclosed holes, recolor its shell by a fixed source-color mapping, recolor each\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/6/code.py:134:    - Two independent classifications interact: source color decides the shell color only for hollow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:18:#    - The fused variant introduces three visually distinct \"hole-like\" situations inside a blue shell:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:21:#      3) a deep narrow indentation that opens to the shell bbox edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:23:#      old task: blue ring + yellow 3x3 hole -> teal shell, magenta hole, red outer outline.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:28:#      So a nested object becomes teal shell + orange-rimmed magenta core, and a deep notch becomes a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:40:#      top-level objects. `main()` therefore iterates only blue shell components.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:42:#      treats every enclosed pocket of the blue shell as hole-like regardless of whether the pocket is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:47:#    - The outer red outline must be computed from the shell after all hole-like regions are filled into\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:52:#    - Generator placement uses a conservative occupied mask: shell union pocket/notch. This is stricter\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:64:#    - Only three new semantic colors appear in output: teal shell, magenta hole fill, orange hole rim.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:68:#    - The color change is structurally anchored instead of arbitrary: only blue shells with a visible\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:274:    Recolor shell objects with hole-like regions and add both outer and inner outlines.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:277:    - A mostly uniform background contains several separated blue 4-connected outer shells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:278:    - Some blue shells enclose a rectangular pocket that is either empty background or fully filled\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:279:      by a green nested object, and some shells instead expose a deep narrow notch from one bbox edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:282:    - Every blue outer shell receives a one-cell 8-neighborhood red outline on background cells only.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:283:    - If a shell has any enclosed pocket or qualifying deep notch, recolor the blue shell to teal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:285:      rim to orange, and compute the red outer outline on the shell after those regions are treated as filled.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:288:    - The transform is driven by the geometry of the blue shell rather than the original fill of the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:298:        shell_mask = _coords_mask(g.shape, coords)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:299:        pockets = _interior_pockets(shell_mask, coords)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:300:        deep_notches = _deep_notch_regions(shell_mask, coords)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:303:        solid = shell_mask.copy()\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:305:            out[shell_mask] = SPECIAL_BODY_COLOR\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:492:def _make_shell_with_pocket(target_pocket_area: int) -> np.ndarray | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:562:        outer = _make_shell_with_pocket(random.choice(POCKET_VALID_AREAS))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:704:        shell = _coords_mask(g.shape, coords)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:705:        for pocket in _interior_pockets(shell, coords):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/34/code.py:711:        deep += len(_deep_notch_regions(shell, coords))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/36/code.py:18:#    recolored with two interior colors (teal shell + magenta hole fill), and all\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/36/code.py:46:#        important because color can no longer distinguish shell vs filled hole.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/36/code.py:179:    detection the shell and its interior hole fill collapse to the same teal color;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/20/code.py:257:    - The contour geometry is still the original outer shell, but two cues now interact:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/13/code.py:18:#    8-neighborhood outline, and hollow ones also changed shell/hole colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/13/code.py:71:#    hollow shell, and hole fill. The chained pair block exposes their mapping,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/13/code.py:228:    assert np.all(inner[[0, 2], :] == hollow_color), \"hollow icon shell malformed\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/13/code.py:229:    assert np.all(inner[:, [0, 2]] == hollow_color), \"hollow icon shell malformed\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/13/code.py:284:    - Detect the legend orientation and decode from it the source object color, outline color, hollow-shell color, and hole-fill color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/12/code.py:30:#    (solid=blue, hollow shell=teal, hole=magenta), while the right group uses a muted warm scheme\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/12/code.py:31:#    (solid=gray, hollow shell=brown, hole=magenta). Second, outline colors ignore groups and are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/12/code.py:205:    - Apply a group-specific interior palette: left solids stay blue (1), left hollow shells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/12/code.py:206:      become teal (8); right solids become gray (5), right hollow shells become brown (9);\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/2/code.py:20:#    - Original behavior on a hollow object was: recolor the shell teal, recolor the hole magenta,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/2/code.py:27:#      original -> teal shell + magenta hole + red exterior halo;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/2/code.py:28:#      combo -> outer shell cells become red, inner shell cells stay teal, and the enclosed hole\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/2/code.py:38:#      at least one hollow component with both a preserved hole and a surviving teal shell core, plus\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/2/code.py:47:#      in a boolean mask. Without that step, shell cells touching the yellow cavity would turn red and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/2/code.py:77:#    - The preserved hole is inferable because it is enclosed by recolored shell pixels, not because it\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/10/code.py:28:#    - Example trace: a 5x5 ring with a 3x3 hole used to become teal shell + full magenta 3x3\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/aa4ec2a5/10/code.py:29:#      hole + full red outside outline. Now it becomes teal shell + yellow 8-cell hole rim +\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/d35bdbdc/D5/0/code.py:17:#   variant outputs border 7 / center 7, so the whole visible shell changes and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/d35bdbdc/D5/0/code.py:192:    - The center pixel of each destination is still the lookup token, but the transferred attribute is now the outer shell, so the visible change is a border recoloring rather than a center replacement.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D10/0/code.py:22:#    a marker at that cell therefore changes, and the filled shells bend differently.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D10/0/code.py:182:    The markers do not label absolute positions; they define a repeating palette over taxicab-distance shells, so irregular regions reveal bent, diamond-like layers instead of the old 8-neighbor framing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D10/1/code.py:172:    The markers still define a repeating palette, but the layer index now comes from Euclidean distance, so large irregular regions turn into rounded concentric bands instead of purely rectangular shells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D10/2/code.py:16:#   The old task read each region as a stack of rectangular shells measured from\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D10/2/code.py:26:#   color every cell from its boundary shell number; output is rectangular frames.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D10/2/code.py:136:    The markers no longer describe how far inward a boundary shell lies; instead each\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D7/0/code.py:9:      每对相邻框之间是一圈矩形环带，最内层框内部是一个中心矩形区域。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/13e47133/D7/0/code.py:34:    - main() 中 `_cc4(free)` 默认每个区域是 4 连通块；嵌套矩形环带确实是单个 4 连通块。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:26:#    - Variant: the object becomes \"core + 1-cell shell\". The shell is mostly the fill color, but\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:27:#      exactly two opposite shell corners are recolored as guide markers, e.g. TL=2 and BR=7.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:34:#      until object's red shell-corner sits at TL and blue shell-corner sits at BR -> crop the whole\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:35:#      1-cell shell and keep the core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:45:#      new guide signal is only at shell corners.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:47:#      shell whose informative pixels happen to be at opposite corners.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:49:#      a filled rectangle with a 1-cell shell; only the shell corners can differ from the shell fill.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:58:#      full edges. Because the core is asymmetric and the shell markers move to different corners,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:107:    Align a shell-framed rectangle to diagonal corner controls via dihedral transforms.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:111:    - A central filled rectangle with a 1-cell shell; two opposite shell corners are recolored as guides.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/fc7cae8d/D1/5/code.py:115:    - Remove the outer 1-cell shell and output the core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/db0c5428/D14/1/code.py:132:      the first 5x5 shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8b7bacbf/D1/1/code.py:19:#     - 被填充对象改成“闭角矩形环”，因为原题的缺角环在 8 连通背景下会漏到外部，\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8b7bacbf/D1/1/code.py:29:#     - 一个闭角矩形环内部若有对角链 `# . / . #` 形式的同色墙：\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8b7bacbf/D1/1/code.py:41:#     - 闭角矩形环与内部对角隔墙全部使用同一种 frame_color。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/3e6067c3/D1/0/code.py:57:# - 假设每个有效方块的边框是单色矩形环，内部是单色实心矩形。generator 用 `_draw_frame()` 保证。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8b7bacbf/D17/1/code.py:3:# Change: 圆角矩形环——矩形边框去除四个角像素，腔体为内部矩形区域 → 菱形/旋转45度的框架环\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8b7bacbf/D17/1/code.py:24:        - 矩形环的 cavity 是一个小矩形, boundary 也是单色\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/d35bdbdc/D7/1/code.py:17:      - 生成器固定构造一个位于中部的细矩形环, 在 8 邻域下连成单个闭合灰色分量\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/d35bdbdc/D7/1/code.py:33:      - 若灰色是中央矩形环, 上/右/下三个精灵分别各有一个端口落在环上\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D18/0/code.py:19:#    - 小例子：若输入中央有一个蓝色空心矩形环，原 solver 会因为它不碰边缘而把它当装饰，洪填会穿过它；\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D18/0/code.py:22:#    - 若允许任意复杂闭环与任意装饰，少量样例很难归纳；因此生成器固定为 1-3 个“空心矩形环”结构墙。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D18/0/code.py:30:#    - 结构墙采用 1 像素厚矩形环，且与网格四边至少隔 3 格，保证它真的是“浮动”而非借边围栏。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:22:#   whole chamber as step-by-step BFS shells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:40:# - A fixed shell palette of seven colors is used. Seven is enough to show a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:44:#   least five visible distance layers and at least four distinct shell colors,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:75:# - The shell palette depends only on BFS step number, never on invisible color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:77:# - Because accepted samples must show multiple shells, a human sees outward\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:83:#   shell colors are reserved for reachable background only. This keeps the three\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:86:#   the shell pattern.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:92:#   several early shells are visible before any repeat, which is the part humans\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:219:    Render the marker-reachable chamber as iterative distance shells while removing hidden blue ornaments.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D10/1/code.py:229:    - Erase blue decorations stranded on the blocked side, then color each newly reached background cell by its flood step, producing ordered shell bands around the marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8b7bacbf/D11/2/code.py:43:#    干扰网络仍采用细线 + 小矩形环，和主对象同语法；同时通过 1-cell 以上间隔避免不同网络糊成一团。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D10/2/code.py:15:#    - The old upper sloped ramp is replaced by one floating pipe shell in rows 1..5.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D10/2/code.py:16:#      The shell is a single-color solid component that does not touch the left/right border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D10/2/code.py:17:#    - Inside the shell there is a covered cavity: a 2x3 source chamber feeding a short\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D10/2/code.py:26:#      inside a covered shell. The old local rule is unchanged: bottom-row source water slides\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D10/2/code.py:54:#    - The source water is visibly enclosed by a roofed shell, which makes the D10 change clear\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/28a6681f/D10/2/code.py:151:    - 0 is background and 1 is water. The other colors are rigid basin walls plus one upper pipe shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D6/3/code.py:49:#    Frames are still 4x4 shells around unique 2x2 blocks and keep the original\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dd6b8c4b/D9/1/code.py:26:#       corridor cells and an outer source region. Consecutive square shells would touch and different opening positions would\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dd6b8c4b/D17/0/code.py:16:#       - deterministic tie break inside the same distance shell is row-major\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D5/3/code.py:23:#   - To keep the D5 change observable, the generator makes emitter shells tall enough that their\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D5/3/code.py:39:#   - Every emitter shell fits strictly inside the left pane and reaches sep-1 only after the output-first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D5/3/code.py:259:    \"\"\"Build a closed emitter shell whose hole height is odd, so its center row is unambiguous.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D5/2/code.py:11:#      at `sep-2`; under the original rule it would emit, but in this variant it stays silent. A 4x3 shell (2x1 hole) or 3x4\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D5/2/code.py:12:#      shell (1x2 hole) still emits because its enclosed hole area is at least 2.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D5/2/code.py:206:def _rect_shell(hole_h: int, hole_w: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D5/2/code.py:225:    return _rect_shell(hole_h, hole_w)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D5/2/code.py:229:    return _rect_shell(1, 1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D19/2/code.py:31:      A rectangular black shell with a background hole touching the inner separator wall slides right,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D19/2/code.py:34:      The same shell slides right, but its enclosed cavity contains both background and a visible color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D19/2/code.py:47:    - The shell geometry stays close to the original task, so D19 is the main novelty rather than a new\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D19/2/code.py:181:    - After sliding, find enclosed non-black cavities inside the black shells; only cavities that touch the column just left of the wall can emit.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D2/0/code.py:275:    \"\"\"Build an emitter sprite patch: a closed shell with an interior hole at the right edge.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D8/0/code.py:25:#         step1: erase the old 3x4 shell, redraw it flush against the separator\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D8/0/code.py:28:#         step1: keep the old shell, also draw the flush copy\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D8/2/code.py:246:    \"\"\"Build an emitter sprite patch: a closed shell with an interior hole at the right edge.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D1/0/code.py:257:    \"\"\"Original orientation: closed shell with an interior hole at the right edge.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/2/code.py:25:#         \"large shell containing a small sprite\" instead of one flat color pattern\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/2/code.py:43:#       1. outer shell = group identity and spatial frame\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/2/code.py:71:#     prototype; here that would wrongly overwrite the outer shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/2/code.py:74:#     occupied extents; in this variant the outer shell is preserved, so bbox alignment\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/2/code.py:101:#     every outer-A shell receives the inner layout seen inside outer-B shells, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/c7f57c3e/D4/2/code.py:232:    Swap the inner micro-sprite between the two outer-shell groups while preserving each shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D1/1/code.py:24:#      * Variant: the same ring is effectively transposed into a bottom-facing shell. After\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D3/1/code.py:261:    \"\"\"Build a closed shell whose triggering hole has width 1 or 2.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D2/1/code.py:54:#      shell should control docking. We therefore switch to connected\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D7/3/code.py:73:      \"already full\", \"missing head\", and \"empty shell\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D17/0/code.py:21:#     - Original: a black shell slides right, its hole reaches `sep-2`, and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D17/0/code.py:23:#     - Variant: a blue shell slides right, the same hole reaches `sep-2`, and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D17/0/code.py:24:#       solver paints blue on those rows; a green shell in another band paints green.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D17/0/code.py:65:#   - The mapping is local and explicit: ray color = color of the shell containing\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D14/1/code.py:23:#       original regime: left_w=8, right_w=3, a 4-wide shell can shift several cells,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D14/1/code.py:25:#       variant regime: left_w=4, right_w=9, the same idea becomes a 3-wide mini-shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D14/1/code.py:35:#     keeps the rule readable from a few examples: tiny shells on the left, long rays\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D14/1/code.py:108:    Key insight: The D14 variant compresses the sprites into miniature shells, but the trigger is unchanged: only sealed pockets pressed against the separator side fire, and the now-wide right pane makes those rays the dominant output feature.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D14/1/code.py:241:    \"\"\"Choose non-adjacent internal divider rows for a 3-wide emitter shell.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D14/1/code.py:257:    \"\"\"Build a tiny closed shell whose 1-cell cavity touches the trigger column when flush.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D3/0/code.py:19:#      colors taken from 3 different bars: outer shell, middle layer, core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D10/3/code.py:212:    \"\"\"Build an emitter sprite patch: a closed shell with an interior hole at the right edge.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/0/code.py:18:# - In the original task, some sprites were closed shells that produced rays after sliding,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/0/code.py:20:# - In this variant, every sprite is a closed shell emitter. After sliding flush to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/0/code.py:50:#   closed shells, so the triggering cavity is always a true hole.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/0/code.py:120:    Key insight: The task no longer asks which sprites are real emitters; every sprite is built to become a nozzle after sliding, so the only reasoning is \"slide first, then read hole rows to the right of each shell.\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/0/code.py:216:    \"\"\"Rows whose cavity touches the shell's right wall and will reach sep-2 when flush.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/0/code.py:249:    \"\"\"Build an emitter sprite patch: a closed shell with a hole that touches the right wall.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/7c66cb00/D17/1/code.py:30:#   Because red equals the bar fill, the outer shell blends into the bar and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/1/code.py:15:#     grey separator, then enclosed background holes that touch the inner face of the shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/1/code.py:22:#     The solver still scans all holes, but only the single true shell contributes rows whose\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/1/code.py:26:#   - Distractors are biased toward near-miss shapes, especially \"false-hole\" shells that do\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/1/code.py:106:    Key insight: The answer is not based on counting sprites but on isolating the single shell whose enclosed pocket reaches the separator-facing side after the slide; lookalikes either stay open to the outside or keep their pocket one column too far left.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/1/code.py:215:    \"\"\"Closed shell whose pocket reaches the separator-facing inner wall when flush.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D11/1/code.py:266:    \"\"\"Closed shell with a pocket that stops one column short of the triggering position.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/bf45cf4b/D16/1/code.py:159:    - The input foreground is a single 8-connected nested structure.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D7/1/code.py:29:# - Example trace: if a sprite becomes a 5x4 rectangular shell after sliding, its enclosed hole spans\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D7/1/code.py:30:#   rows 1..3 inside the shell, so the hole height is 3. The original task would paint every cell in\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6e453dd6/D7/1/code.py:40:#     That still holds because emitters are closed shells and distractors are open/solid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e3721c99/D4/1/code.py:35:#    - The output has at most three layers in any region: background, grey shell, and one interior color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/20270e3b/D4/0/code.py:347:    shell = _dilate4(socket) & (~socket)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/20270e3b/D4/0/code.py:349:    shell[top_pad - 2, conn_cols] = True\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/20270e3b/D4/0/code.py:352:    base[shell] = int(OBJ)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/67e490f4/D4/2/code.py:53:#   inside a sprite bbox. In the variant this must be replaced with hollow-shell rendering.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/67e490f4/D4/2/code.py:67:#   solid silhouettes. The consistent rule is \"ignore the hollow interior; use the outer shell\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/1818057f/D11/2/code.py:123:    - This is no longer a local '+' template check. The solver first groups pixels by connectivity, then extracts each component's outer shell; only those boundary pixels change color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:30:#   solver instead inspects its square bbox and detects shell insets [0, 3, 5, 8],\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:33:#   to \"square-border layers directly visible as nested shells\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:37:#   have 1-6 layers with varying shell gaps and varying overall size.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:47:#   That value could accidentally correlate with nested-shell depth, but the new solver\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:51:#   generator ensures only the true object colors yield square-shell evidence.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:58:#   overlaps/noise. Generator therefore protects shell corners and side midpoints from\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:60:# - Equal-count objects are forced to overlap on non-protected shell pixels so cover\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:67:# - Sorting never depends on raw color ids. The only ordering cue is visible shell count,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:71:# - Noise is kept off frame pixels. This avoids turning thin shells into unreadable\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:73:#   color. Overlaps are limited to a small fraction of shell pixels to keep objects\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:88:# - Every true shell keeps all four corners visible.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:89:# - Every true shell keeps enough side pixels for threshold-based perimeter detection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:90:# - At most one tie pair exists, and tied objects overlap on shell pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:151:def _detect_shell_insets(mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[list[int], np.ndarray]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:189:    insets, _cropped = _detect_shell_insets(g == int(color))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:193:def _shells_for_color(g: np.ndarray, color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:200:    insets, cropped = _detect_shell_insets(mask)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:204:    shell_mask = np.zeros_like(cropped, dtype=bool)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:206:        _paint_square_border(shell_mask, int(inset))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:209:    out[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1] = shell_mask\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:218:    above: dict[int, set[int]] = {color: set() for color, _layers, _shells in group}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:219:    indegree: dict[int, int] = {color: 0 for color, _layers, _shells in group}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:259:    - The number of detected square shells is that color's count value.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:260:    - The color with zero detected shells is the pad color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:261:    - Sort real drawing colors by shell count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed shell set lies underneath the other.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:264:    - The meaningful quantity is no longer the number of rooms inside a line grid. Each true object directly encodes its value as the number of nested same-color square borders, while the scattered pad color never forms a coherent square shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:273:        shells = _shells_for_color(g, int(color))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:274:        counts.append((int(color), int(layers), shells))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:276:    drawings = [(color, layers, shells) for color, layers, shells in counts if layers > 0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:277:    pad_colors = [color for color, layers, _shells in counts if layers == 0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:283:    for layers in sorted({layers for _color, layers, _shells in drawings}):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:287:    width = max(layers for _color, layers, _shells in ordered)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:289:    for r, (color, layers, _shells) in enumerate(ordered):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:417:    shell_a = int(frame_a[2].sum())\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:418:    shell_b = int(frame_b[2].sum())\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:419:    allowed = max(1, math.floor(_MAX_OVERLAP_SHARE * min(shell_a, shell_b)))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:504:        shell_arr = frames[idx][2]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:506:        rs, cs = np.where(shell_arr)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:512:        shell_arr = frames[idx][2]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:514:        rs, cs = np.where(shell_arr)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:554:        shell_mask, protected = built\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D3/2/code.py:555:        frames.append((int(color), int(layers), shell_mask, protected))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D3/3/code.py:189:    - The frame keeps the original outer shell and left-wall opening, but now also contains a horizontal internal divider. Two target columns pierce that divider; two stay entirely below it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D5/0/code.py:12:#     new: teal 模板的几何中心（这里具体化为模板包围盒中心）对齐到散落 source 像素\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/abc82100/D5/0/code.py:17:#     - 输出时，不再把 legend 的 anchor 挪到散落 source 上，而是把 teal 模板包围盒中心挪到\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e8686506/D10/1/code.py:3:# Change: 最大碎片为环形/U形等非凸形状（中心有洞或凹陷），其他小碎片填入空隙... → 框架碎片为完整矩形环\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D5/3/code.py:41:#      enough that humans can see three nested regions: teal shell, orange hole rim, magenta core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D5/2/code.py:55:#      stay at least one cell away from the outer boundary and from each other, the shell remains\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e8686506/D16/2/code.py:13:#       - The visible result is a clean nested structure: outer corners + outer edges, inner corners + inner edges,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/0/code.py:49:#     multi-hole components are generated as one connected outer shell with 1-cell minimum walls\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:30:#         area <= 3  -> shell teal (8), hole magenta (6)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:31:#         area > 3   -> shell green (3), hole yellow (4)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:34:#     - original logic: a 1x3 hole and a 2x2 hole both trigger the same recolor, shell=8 and hole=6\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:35:#     - variant logic: the 1x3 hole has total area 3, so shell=8 and hole=6; the 2x2 hole has total area 4, so shell=3 and hole=4\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:46:#   - the old \"if holes: out[mask] = 8\" relied on all hollow objects sharing one shell color; this is no longer safe\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:58:#   - the large-hole branch uses a different shell color (green) even though its hole is yellow like the background,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:134:    - Total hole area <= 3 gives teal shell (8) with magenta holes (6); total hole area > 3 gives green shell (3) with yellow holes (4).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:151:            shell_color, hole_color = _hole_colors(total_hole_area)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D2/1/code.py:152:            out[mask] = shell_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D1/0/code.py:26:#     - 1-2 components are hollow and still trigger teal(8) shell + magenta(6) hole recoloring\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D6/3/code.py:18:#    - The wall is now a rectangular shell around a cavity. Exactly one side of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D6/3/code.py:19:#      the shell is 1 cell thick; the other three sides are 2 or 3 cells thick.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D6/3/code.py:50:#    - The shell is an axis-aligned rectangle ring, so it never self-overlaps.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D6/3/code.py:53:#    - Obstacles are placed only on background cells outside the shell and at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D6/3/code.py:320:    - bg is the most common color. The wall is the most frequent non-bg color and forms one closed shell around a cavity.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D6/3/code.py:458:def _render_shell(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D6/3/code.py:586:    wall_mask, interior = _render_shell(grid, rad, r0, c0, inner_h, inner_w, t, wall_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D13/1/code.py:74:    - bg is the dominant color. The wall is now two-toned: a large closed-color shell plus one straight open-color face that seals the former gap.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D13/1/code.py:75:    - A small fill-color blob sits inside the shell near the closed end. Other non-bg colors are sparse obstacles.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D1/2/code.py:27:#      original logic first recolors the shell to teal and the hole to magenta, then computes the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D1/2/code.py:28:#      contour from \"shell + hole\" so the hole does not receive a red ring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D1/2/code.py:30:#      solid mask; therefore only the outer shell edge turns red, while hole-adjacent shell pixels\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D1/2/code.py:57:#    - Hollow objects still use only three output colors locally: red boundary, teal shell interior,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D2/0/code.py:28:#     7 -> add the radius-2 shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D2/0/code.py:29:#     3 -> add radii 3 and 4 shell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D2/1/code.py:63:#    plus-like nested structure instead of a filled square. Even two-layer\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/1/code.py:19:#     hollow_rule: only hollow components are recolored, and the shell color depends on the component's input color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/1/code.py:25:#     use exactly three source colors and three shell targets so the mapping is finite and learnable\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/1/code.py:26:#     generate exactly two hollow objects per grid so each example shows at least two color->shell pairs\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/1/code.py:38:#     shell targets {blue->teal, green->gray, orange->maroon} are distinct from bg=yellow, outline=red, and hole=magenta\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/1/code.py:40:#     no extra markers or overlays are introduced; only object shells, interior holes, and external outlines remain visible\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/1/code.py:94:    Outline each colored object, while hollow ones transfer to a color-specific shell color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/1/code.py:103:    - Hollow objects recolor their shell by a fixed source-color mapping, fill enclosed holes with magenta (6), then receive the same red outline.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/1/code.py:106:    shell replacement used for hollow objects, while holes are merged into the solid mask before outlining.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D8/2/code.py:18:#      outer shell. Their placement order is top->down, so the topmost even stripe\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D8/2/code.py:24:#      variant outer shell from evens top->down = 4,2\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D8/2/code.py:31:#      phases are present and the even-shell ordering matters.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D8/2/code.py:46:#    - Requiring at least two even-position stripes ensures the top->down shell rule\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D8/2/code.py:108:    - Even-position stripes (2nd, 4th, 6th, ...) build the shell in top->down order.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D8/2/code.py:120:    shell_thickness = sum(height for _col, height in even_runs)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D8/2/code.py:121:    final_n = inner_size + 2 * shell_thickness\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:19:#     - Every top-level object still has a blue outer shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:20:#     - 1-2 of those blue shells are \"nested\": the shell encloses one interior pocket, but the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:22:#     - The green inner component fills the entire enclosed pocket, so the shell geometry is still\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:27:#     - For nested objects, recolor only the outer blue shell to teal, keep the green inner object\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:28:#       unchanged, and outline the occupied union of shell + inner object.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:31:#   - Original local situation: blue shell surrounds a yellow hole -> output teal shell, magenta\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:33:#   - Variant local situation: the same shell surrounds a green inner object -> output teal shell,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:39:#   - Keep the original object count, shell irregularity, and placement spacing, so the puzzle still\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:47:#   - `_interior_regions` still works because the outer shell is detected from blue cells alone; the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:48:#     enclosed pocket is defined by shell geometry, not by the pocket's fill color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:49:#   - `main()` only iterates blue components. This assumes every top-level object has a blue shell and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:50:#     every non-blue component is nested inside one of those shells. `generate_input()` enforces that.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:52:#     would be overwritten by red when they touch the shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:53:#   - The original code's \"any enclosed non-shell region behaves like a hole\" is no longer valid:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:57:#   - Generation places whole occupied masks (outer shell plus any inner object), not just the blue\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:58:#     shell, so components cannot overlap or violate the intended spacing after nesting is added.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:59:#   - Nested shells are produced from the original hollow-shell builder, then the enclosed pocket is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:64:#     keep that core while recoloring only the shell. This is a direct visual contrast with solid objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:68:#   - A generated sample is considered non-degenerate when it contains at least one blue shell with an\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:155:    - If a blue component encloses another colored object, recolor only the blue shell to teal (8).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:156:    - Keep the enclosed inner object in its original color, and compute the outline around the occupied union of shell and inner object.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:158:    Key insight: The shell geometry still defines the enclosed pocket, but the pocket is no longer a hole to fill; it is an existing inner object that must be preserved while the shell is recolored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:337:def _make_nested_shell(target_pocket_area: int) -> np.ndarray | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D3/3/code.py:398:        outer = _make_nested_shell(random.choice(POCKET_VALID_AREAS))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/45a5af55/D3/1/code.py:26:#                1px color-5 frame, so the total shell is 5 cells per side instead of 3, and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D4/1/code.py:23:#    rectangular background holes. The local hollow handling is unchanged: hollow shells become\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D13/0/code.py:20:#    their shell and hole. The change is that the palette rule is no longer only\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D13/0/code.py:69:#    hollow shell, magenta hole). Their roles are tied to distinct miniature\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D13/0/code.py:243:      the hollow-object shell color, the hole color, and the outline color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/aa4ec2a5/D13/0/code.py:246:    - If an object encloses a background hole, recolor its shell and hole with the legend's hollow colors,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:19:#   group: a large hollow rectangular shell contains a smaller hollow shell, and a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:21:# - The large shell's empty interior is split into two visually distinct enclosed bg\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:22:#   regions by the inner hollow shell:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:23:#   1. an annulus between the outer border and the inner shell,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:24:#   2. the inner shell's own hole.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:25:# - The output fills the annulus using the outer shell's palette mapping. The inner\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:26:#   hole is then filled only if the inner-shell color also appears as a palette key.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:27:#   If the inner-shell color is a distractor, its hole stays bg.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:32:#   encloses this bg region as its parent shell? For the annulus, that parent is the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:33:#   outer shell; for the inner hole, it is the inner shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:36:# - The main signal is still one nested hollow shell, but the optional extra nested\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:41:# - The inner shell has two repeated modes across samples: mapped or distractor. That\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:49:#   evidence. In the nested case, processing order should not change which shell owns a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:56:# - The generator reserves a 1-cell moat between the palette and the outer shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:57:# - The outer shell is kept at least 1 cell away from the global boundary so its hole\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:59:# - The inner shell is placed strictly inside the outer hole with at least 1 bg cell of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:70:# - The outer fill stops cleanly at the inner shell border, so the containment relation\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:75:#   shell, the inner hollow shell, and one small same-colored nested sprite. No extra\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:77:# - The outer shell stays rectangular for readability; the smaller shell is slightly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:82:#   by the visible edge palette and the shell color that encloses the region.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:83:# - The inner shell's distractor mode is also visible: its color is absent from the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:87:# - The solver assumes each relevant shell is a single connected non-bg component. The\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:90:#   generator creates proper nesting, so the immediate parent shell is always the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:91:#   smallest enclosing shell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:94:#   the palette to the left or right edge so the tall nested structure fits reliably.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:372:    as a wall by itself. This resolves nested shells. If nothing encloses the region,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:419:    nearest enclosing shell rather than by raw adjacency frequency.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:440:    - The play area contains a large hollow shell with a smaller hollow shell inside it, so one enclosed outer cavity is split into layered interior regions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:445:    - For nested interiors, assign each enclosed region to the nearest shell that truly contains it, then fill that region with the shell's mapped color if its border color appears in the palette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:448:    - Adjacency alone is no longer enough because an outer cavity can touch both the large shell and the inner shell; the correct fill comes from containment depth, not just from the most frequent neighboring color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:470:# Nested-shell geometry\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:525:    \"\"\"Pick palette keys/fills and the outer/inner shell colors.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D11/1/code.py:548:    \"\"\"Candidate placements for the outer shell, leaving one bg cell to the frame.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D4/1/code.py:81:#       the solver must use the nested structure\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D15/1/code.py:16:#     - New flow: build the same forbidden cells, also record the exact boundary shell at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D15/1/code.py:18:#       cells on that shell yellow instead of green.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D15/1/code.py:32:#       variant must inspect whether each path cell lies on the boundary shell first.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cb2d8a2c/D15/1/code.py:34:#       expose the exact shell at radius + 1 without changing passability.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D4/2/code.py:32:#     - The lookup key is the majority color over the entire border shell touching the hole,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dbff022c/D4/2/code.py:39:#     - Therefore the old local rule and the new majority-over