ode.py:68:   - Ejection rays are reserved globally so no cup body, arrow, or later ray occupies another ray.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:73:   - Across examples a human sees: empty rectangular cups, same-color arrows attached near the closed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:76:   - Direction is not encoded by numeric color id; it is visible from local arrow placement.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:83:   - The arrow sits off the ejection ray, so it remains visible in both input and output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:87:     directly visible: the cup geometry is rectangular, direction is a local arrow marker, and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:92:   - Assumption: each relevant border component contains exactly one removable 3-cell L arrow whose\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:93:     removal yields an exact rectangular cup. Guaranteed by `_attach_arrow`.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:94:   - Assumption: the arrow lies completely outside exactly one side of the recovered cup bbox. Guaranteed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:95:     by the fixed arrow templates.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:190:def _enumerate_arrow_candidates(border_set: set[tuple[int, int]]) -> list[set[tuple[int, int]]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:243:def _arrow_side(arrow_cells: set[tuple[int, int]], cup_bbox: tuple[int, int, int, int]) -> str | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:244:    ar0, ac0, ar1, ac1 = bbox_coords(np.array(sorted(arrow_cells), dtype=int))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:272:    \"\"\"Recover the cup body by stripping one arrow candidate from the merged component.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:274:    for arrow_cells in _enumerate_arrow_candidates(border_set):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:275:        cup_set = border_set - arrow_cells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:280:        tail_dir = _arrow_side(arrow_cells, cup_bbox)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:299:                \"arrow_cells\": set(arrow_cells),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:335:    - A same-color 3-pixel arrow attached beside the closed side tells the ejection direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:472:def _attach_arrow(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:479:    \"\"\"Attach the same-color L arrow beside the closed side of the rectangular cup.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:481:    arrow_template = ARROW_OFFSETS[direction][int(rng.integers(0, 2))]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:482:    arrow_coords = [(spike[0] + dr, spike[1] + dc) for dr, dc in arrow_template]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:484:    all_coords = border_coords + arrow_coords\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:493:    for r, c in arrow_coords:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:536:    \"\"\"Place the combined cup+arrow patch away from the border with a clear reserved ray.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/37/code.py:664:    patch, spike, cup_bbox_local = _attach_arrow(rng, patch_body, spike_body, direction, border_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/24/code.py:30:#    This makes the combo visually distinct from the original narrow markers without\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/689c358e.py:73:    Mark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/689c358e.py:77:    - Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:6:Fusion variant of 3dc255db: rectangular 3-sided cups plus detached arrow markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:18:     detached same-color 3-pixel L arrow sits just outside one of the frame's closed sides. The\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:19:     mouth is determined from the open side; the spike is the center pixel of the arrow-indicated\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:21:   - Example: a 5x5 frame open downward can now eject upward, leftward, or rightward. If the arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:28:   - The arrow vocabulary is small: a detached 3-cell L triomino, with two offset variants per\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:36:     border color is split across a frame component and a detached arrow component.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:38:     opening only determines the mouth; the arrow chooses among the three closed sides.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:40:     side, not from the side opposite the arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:43:       2. the associated arrow is a detached 3-cell 2x2-L component of the same color;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:44:       3. the arrow lies exactly one blank cell beyond one frame side and overlaps that side's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:50:   - Generator builds the output state first: cup frame + detached arrow + ejected line.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:51:   - Combined occupied cells of cup and arrow keep an 8-neighborhood gap from other cups/arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:54:   - The arrow is offset away from the ray, so the output line never overwrites the marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:58:   - The detached arrow is plainly visible on one closed side, so humans can see that ejection is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:65:   - Cups, arrows, and rays are kept disjoint across objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:69:   - No new color mapping is introduced. The arrow reuses the frame color, so the association is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:76:   - Assumption: exactly one nearby L-arrow of the same color lies one cell beyond a closed side.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:77:     Guaranteed by fixed arrow templates and unique border colors per cup.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:211:def _is_l_arrow_component(cells: set[tuple[int, int]]) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:212:    \"\"\"3-cell detached arrow sprite: an L triomino inside a 2x2 bbox.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:232:def _arrow_side(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:233:    arrow_cells: set[tuple[int, int]],\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:236:    \"\"\"Infer which cup side the detached arrow marks.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:237:    ar0, ac0, ar1, ac1 = (int(v) for v in bbox_coords(np.array(sorted(arrow_cells), dtype=int)))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:255:    - A detached same-color 3-pixel L arrow picks one closed side as the tail.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:273:        arrow_candidates: list[set[tuple[int, int]]] = []\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:276:            if _is_l_arrow_component(comp_set):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:277:                arrow_candidates.append(comp_set)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:315:            for arrow_cells in arrow_candidates:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:316:                tail_dir = _arrow_side(arrow_cells, bbox)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:363:def _attach_arrow(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:370:    \"\"\"Return a combined patch containing the frame and a detached arrow.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:378:    arrow_template = ARROW_OFFSETS[tail_dir][int(rng.integers(0, 2))]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:379:    arrow_coords = [(spike[0] + dr, spike[1] + dc) for dr, dc in arrow_template]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:380:    all_coords = body_coords + arrow_coords\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:390:    for r, c in arrow_coords:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:430:    \"\"\"Place one cup+arrow combo while preserving spacing and a clear ray to the edge.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/7/code.py:538:    combo_patch, spike, mouth, body_bbox_local = _attach_arrow(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/16b78196/21/code.py:41:#      examples for both arrow markers and terminal sprites, but the two roles are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/16b78196/21/code.py:44:#    - Decoy notches remain present, so the arrow is not decorative.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/16b78196/21/code.py:86:#    - Only 1-2 small arrow markers are added, and each is placed adjacent to one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/898e7135/53/code.py:126:    assert w >= 2, \"grid too narrow for color legend\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:11:# In the input, you will see several objects of the same color that are in an arrowhead shape and facing different directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:12:# The goal is to find the directions of the arrowheads and draw lines that would represent the path they had been moving in to go in that direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:16:    Extend each small arrowhead with a line in the direction it points.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:19:    - The input is a black grid with several same-colored arrowhead objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:20:    - Each arrowhead is a small shape whose missing corner indicates its direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:21:    - Lines should use the arrowhead color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:24:    - Crop each arrowhead and compare the mean positions of colored and black cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:26:    - Draw a line from the matching outer corner of the arrowhead's bounding box to the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:29:    - The arrowhead asymmetry identifies both the direction of travel and the corner where the path begins.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:37:    # for each object, find the direction the arrowhead is pointing in by finding the relative mean position of colored and black pixels in the bounding box of the object\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:60:        # find the direction the arrowhead is pointing in, it is from the mean position of the colored pixels to the mean position of the black pixels\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:63:        # draw a line in the direction the arrowhead is pointing in from the corresponding corner of the bounding box\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:70:            # check if the corner is in the direction that the arrowhead is pointing\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:81:    # choose the color of the