ntroid; the anchor gives the vertical half, the connector gives the horizontal half, and only the pair resolves the correct quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:665:def _centroid_cell_id(mask: np.ndarray, side: int) -> int:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:682:    centroid_ids = [_centroid_cell_id(mask, side) for mask in masks]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:688:        mismatch = sum(int(perm[pi] != centroid_ids[pi]) for pi in range(NUM_PIECES))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:692:        return list(map(int, perm)), anchors, centroid_ids\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:822:def _old_centroid_output(masks: list[np.ndarray], legend_map: np.ndarray, side: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:825:        qid = _centroid_cell_id(mask, side)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:851:    target_ids, anchor_cells_abs, centroid_ids = target_info\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:862:    old_like = _old_centroid_output(masks, legend_map, side)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:886:        \"centroid_ids\": centroid_ids,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/3/code.py:887:        \"mismatch_count\": sum(int(a != b) for a, b in zip(target_ids, centroid_ids)),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/11/code.py:405:    - Tile the four pieces into the unique tight square, then recolor each assembled piece by the target legend color at its centroid quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/11/code.py:43:#    - Free parameters are kept narrow: only three spiral columns vary per path,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/7/code.py:14:#   centroid lands in the top-left quadrant already has source color `d`. That piece looks\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/7/code.py:23:#   unique rectangle inference, and centroid quantization remain valid. The only semantic change in\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/7/code.py:30:#   Every sample keeps 4 distinct palette cells and 4 distinct centroid quadrants. The generator\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/7/code.py:197:    Recolor each piece by quantizing its centroid into a k×k grid, then reading colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/7/code.py:293:        # Determine legend-map cell for each piece by centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/7/code.py:297:            # centroid: average over occupied cells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/7/code.py:334:    - Use each piece centroid to determine which 2×2 quadrant of the assembled square it occupies.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/7/code.py:655:    # Compute centroid quadrant for each piece; all 4 must be distinct.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:31:#      the color-choice hypothesis narrow and repeated across the full training set.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/17/code.py:67:#    - Assumption: each output quadrant corresponds to exactly one piece centroid bucket.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/17/code.py:68:#      Guarantee: generator rejects tilings whose four centroids are not all in different 2x2 cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/17/code.py:71:#      Guarantee: generator writes slot sources from the assembled pieces' actual centroid cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/7/code.py:28:# - The palette remains in the original narrow regime: four non-bg/non-particle\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2d0172a1/D7/1/code.py:17:#     new: each frame ignores band order; its direct children are ranked by centroid distance\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2d0172a1/D7/1/code.py:31:#       - solver breaks ties by child centroid row, then col; generator enforces distinct distances\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2d0172a1/D7/1/code.py:232:    - For siblings inside the same frame, sort them by increasing centroid distance to the parent frame center, then place them as a down-right diagonal chain with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2d0172a1/D7/1/code.py:305:MIN_DIST_DELTA = 2.0           # min squared-distance gap between sibling centroids\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/15/code.py:52:#   must each admit at least one non-canonical D4 orientation; centroid quantization must send the four assembled\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/15/code.py:304:    - After assembly, quantize the four piece centroids into the 2x2 quadrant grid and treat the input piece colors as an implicit legend whose entries come from the diagonally opposite assembled quadrants.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/20/code.py:518:    - Recolor the assembled pieces from that displayed palette according to the piece centroid's quadrant in the final 2×2 layout, and ignore leftover distractors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:20:      - 左边起点表现为 `(row,0)=particle, (row,1)=arrow`; 右边是 `(row,W-1)=particle, (row,W-2)=arrow`;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:118:    \"\"\"Return border particles whose unique adjacent arrow pixel defines the start direction.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:183:    \"\"\"Move arrow-marked edge particles through same-color corridors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:186:    - bg=7, particle=6, arrow marker=2.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:188:    - Particles may appear on any of the four grid edges, each with exactly one adjacent arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:191:    - The arrow offset relative to a border particle gives its initial direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:192:    - The particle moves straight through passable cells (bg, particle, arrow).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:199:      You must first read each edge particle's adjacent arrow to recover the initial\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:630:def _place_particles_and_arrows(g: np.ndarray, starts: list[tuple[int, int, str]]) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:631:    \"\"\"Place particles on the border and their unique adjacent arrow markers.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:633:    arrow_cells: set[tuple[int, int]] = set()\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:641:        if (ar, ac) in arrow_cells:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:645:        arrow_cells.add((ar, ac))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/4/code.py:709:    if not _place_particles_and_arrows(g, starts):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D16/2/code.py:56:#        narrow slit -> dense beam\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/2/code.py:57:#     - The old centroid-to-quadrant recolor rule is invalid under D1/1. main() must instead compute\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/16/code.py:22:#        * tile one rectangle and are recolored by 2x3 / 3x2 centroid cells, or\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/16/code.py:349:def _piece_centroid_cell(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/16/code.py:375:    piece_cells = {pi: _piece_centroid_cell(pl, h, w, (rows, cols)) for pi, pl in chosen.items()}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/16/code.py:405:            rr, cc = _piece_centroid_cell(pl, rect_h, rect_w, (1, 3))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/16/code.py:419:            rr, cc = _piece_centroid_cell(pl, rect_h, rect_w, (3, 1))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/16/code.py:520:      a piece gets the color of the palette cell matching its centroid bucket in the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/28/code.py:82:#   1. narrow lanes move, wide lanes do not;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/28/code.py:89:# - The scene remains a small set of repeated elements: bar rows, narrow/wide\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/28/code.py:263:    \"\"\"Move magenta particles through dashed narrow corridors with bounded gap crossing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/9/code.py:16:#      cover assembly, we still recolor by centroid quadrant, but the final grid\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/9/code.py:47:#      one foreground cell. The generator enforces distinct centroid quadrants\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/9/code.py:398:    - Read the legend strip from left to right as [top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right], convert that into a 2x2 quadrant color map, and recolor each assembled piece by the quadrant containing its centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/21/code.py:45:#    - Original assembly logic assumed geometry or centroid position determined which legend color applied.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/19/code.py:16:#      legend's 2x2 palette by assembled centroid quadrant, and output only A.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/19/code.py:231:    Recolor each piece by quantizing its centroid into a k×k grid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/19/code.py:354:    - Recolor each assembled piece from the palette cell chosen by its centroid position, then rotate the completed square by 90° according to the legend-arm cue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cbebaa4b/D14/0/code.py:39:# - For each tree edge, the parent owns the overlap cells and the child adds two red protrusion cells into them. This\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/42/code.py:13:#      seed; every other selected component is cleared and replaced by one centroid\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/42/code.py:94:    \"\"\"Rounded centroid, snapped to the nearest component cell when necessary.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/42/code.py:116:    \"\"\"Most frequent component color; ties fall back to the color nearest the centroid.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/42/code.py:127:    centroid = np.array([mean_r, mean_c], dtype=float)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/42/code.py:134:        d2 = ((color_pts - centroid) ** 2).sum(axis=1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/42/code.py:192:    - Compress every other matching component to a single centroid pixel colored by that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/4/code.py:389:    - Recolor each assembled piece by the legend's 2x2 core according to the centroid quadrant, but draw\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/cbebaa4b/D14/1/code.py:35:#      the anchor bbox center column in input coordinates.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/5/code.py:40:#      - \"centroid quantization picks palette cells\" is false; palette cells now correspond to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/19/code.py:26:#    - `main()` launch-row detection assumed many bar colors might exist, but its real requirement is narrower:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/29/code.py:22:      - direction is still sign(centroid(red) - centroid(blue))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/29/code.py:57:    arrow\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/29/code.py:96:    color matches and visible arrow orientation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/29/code.py:249:      color match between a side-mounted legend and its chamber wall; the arrow orientation inside that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/28/code.py:23:#      and recolored each piece by the 2x2 legend cell containing its centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/28/code.py:34:#      recolor the 6 regions by row-major legend position. The original centroid-on-rectangle logic is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/28/code.py:49:#    - \"piece color in output comes from centroid quadrant\" fails because the 6 colors are attached to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/28/code.py:50:#      template's explicit logical regions, not derived from centroid quantization.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/12/code.py:114:    center-to-indicator centroid offset.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/18/code.py:119:    \"\"\"Decode border legends and map fill color -> centroid-sign direction.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/13/code.py:17:#   by the legend's 2x2 palette cell selected from its assembled centroid. Here we keep that core step,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/13/code.py:360:      tile the remaining pieces, and recolor each assembled piece from the legend palette using centroid quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/25/code.py:20:#      * The same seed line also touches a U-shaped pocket whose 5-region reaches the top edge through a narrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/43/code.py:395:    - The legend no longer means one atomic arrow. It encodes two cardinal sub-moves, and the fixed processing order is part of the rule: horizontal first, vertical second.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/23/code.py:225:    Recolor each piece by quantizing its centroid into a k×k grid, using legend_map.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/23/code.py:363:    - Anchor the piece nearest the strip at the assembled top-left, and recolor each assembled piece by the legend color selected from the 2x2 quadrant reached by its centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:23:#       - then each mover reads the arrow tied to its own color inside that legend;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:25:#         pink stays at its input cell even if its arrow points into open space.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:33:#       the same visible mover color now selects both which arrow to read and how much of that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:67:#     - Pink is only accepted when its own arrow has at least one open step ahead, so \"pink does\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:257:    Slide multi-colored mover pixels inside sealed wall regions using color-specific arrows and color-specific travel lengths.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:272:    - Wall color chooses which legend applies, while mover color chooses both which arrow to read inside that legend and how much of the available path to traverse.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:353:    raise RuntimeError(\"_sample_legend_dirs: unable to place non-overlapping color-specific arrows\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/26/code.py:78:    - using only cardinal arrows avoids mixing the D6 geometric effect with diagonal corner-cutting\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/26/code.py:88:    - the fused change is inferable from shape geometry plus arrow direction, not from numeric color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/18/code.py:205:def _frame_centroid(br: int, bc: int) -> tuple[float, float]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/18/code.py:210:    cr, cc = _frame_centroid(br, bc)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/18/code.py:211:    orr, occ = _frame_centroid(other_br, other_bc)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/14/code.py:54:#    the assembled piece centroids in the destination bbox, so the human-visible cues remain geometric rather than\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f560132c/14/code.py:442:      bbox quadrant containing that assembled piece's centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2c181942/D1/0/code.py:48:#       - 对每个候选 sprite 尝试 4 个旋转；若某一朝向在某一边出现“support 带全满 + protrusion 带恰等于该边凹口模式”，则视为匹配。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2c181942/D1/0/code.py:573:    protrusions = sum(sum(v) for v in pats.values())\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2c181942/D1/0/code.py:580:        \"hub_protrusions\": int(protrusions),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2c181942/D1/0/code.py:664:    if metrics[\"hub_protrusions\"] < 7:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/e12f9a14/D13/2/code.py:25:#    - The solver still supports all original collision branches. The generator narrows the placement space to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/43/code.py:68:#       combo outputs differ from a narrow unbounded baseline on the same inputs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/27/code.py:78:#     visible legend arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/5/code.py:83:    \"\"\"Rounded centroid, snapped to the nearest component pixel if rounding lands outside.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/5/code.py:117:    centroid = pts.astype(float).mean(axis=0)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/5/code.py:123:        d2 = ((color_pts - centroid) ** 2).sum(axis=1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d59b0160/5/code.py:175:    - Components that pass the test are cleared, replaced by one centroid-like marker pixel\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/33/code.py:67:    color. The direction is `sign(centroid(indicator) - centroid(center))`.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/33/code.py:167:      the arrow-like offset inside the legend.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/2/code.py:26:#      keeps the visual degree of freedom narrow and repeatable across 2-4 ARC examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D9/1/code.py:70:# - Human-visible difference from the original task is strong: outputs are narrow cropped\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:90:def _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr: int, dc: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:97:    patch[_cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc)] = fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:101:CARDINAL_TEMPLATE_MASKS = {(dr, dc): _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc) for dr, dc in CARDINAL_DIRS}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:116:def _match_arrow_direction(fill_mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:127:    Detect border legends and decode direction from a dominant-color 5x5 arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:130:    controls. The dominant-color cells form one of four cardinal arrow templates.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:149:        direction = _match_arrow_direction(patch == fill)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:185:    Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions using border legends drawn as cardinal arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:191:      that forms a visible arrow shape, plus a uniform backing color. The wall color C matches the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:196:      find the legend whose dominant arrow color is also C.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:197:    - Read the arrow orientation directly as one of the four cardinal directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:202:    from the legend's arrow silhouette itself, and every valid movement is purely horizontal or\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/3/code.py:97:    Direction is still sign(centroid(indicator) - centroid(center)).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/3/code.py:233:    - Inside a legend, the center cluster and the outer indicator cluster still define a direction by centroid offset, and the number of indicator pixels is the fixed step count.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/17/code.py:208:      to the coupler centroid, and translate so the couplers land exactly on the station cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/37/code.py:117:    The sign of centroid(indicator) - centroid(center) gives the legend direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:33:       a left token drops through 6, while under the old narrow-gate rule it would have\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:39:     The generator therefore enforces at least two wide gates and at least two narrow gates.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:42:     every wide gate as narrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:66:     bars, and the corresponding output rows show narrow vs wide opened passages around the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:68:   - Because accepted samples must differ from the \"all narrow\" baseline, the width signal is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:383:def _baseline_narrow_gates(gates: list[list[tuple[int, int]]]) -> list[list[tuple[int, int]]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:460:    baseline_opened = _build_opened_grid(_baseline_narrow_gates(gates), width)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:471:    enriched_stats[\"narrow_gates\"] = int(sum(span == NARROW for row in gates for _center, span in row))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:473:    enriched_stats[\"diff_vs_narrow\"] = int(np.count_nonzero(fused_out != baseline_out))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:496:    if int(stats[\"narrow_gates\"]) < 2:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:506:    if int(stats[\"diff_vs_narrow\"]) < 1:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/42/code.py:12:    - border legends still sit on corners and encode a direction by center->indicator centroid\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/2c181942/D11/0/code.py:377:    - after embedding into a 6x6 hub, the inward pixels stay in a narrow central lane,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/16/code.py:59:#   patches have enough room for marker protrusions and body growth.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/42/code.py:21:#      generator narrows legal exits to one corner gap per frame, so every beam is diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/32/code.py:32:        - at a narrow elbow the bridge square exists but the parallel lane disappears, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/32/code.py:35:        - two yellows under the same arrow can end at different depths: one reaches the remote\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/32/code.py:317:    - The wall color still chooses the arrow, but the final resting place now depends on both the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/22/code.py:40:#    - Original assumption: a gate row may have 0-3 gates. The combo narrows this to exactly one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/21/code.py:73:    - Rotate/flip the whole hollow sprite so each embedded marker ends up on the same side of the coupler centroid as the same-colored boundary stripe of the target region.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/15/code.py:269:      movement direction via their centroid offset.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D1/2/code.py:93:def _arrow_template(inner_h: int, inner_w: int, direction: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D1/2/code.py:118:    base = _arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D1/2/code.py:147:        tpl = set(_arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D1/2/code.py:288:    for r, c in _arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/40/code.py:46:  - Room interiors are narrow in the stacking axis and longer in the motion axis, which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:782:def _place_docked_trains(base, bg, regions, label_pool, arrow_color, n_trains, shared_size=None):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:813:                output[indicator_abs] = arrow_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:913:    arrow_color = random.choice(extra_pool)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:914:    label_pool = [c for c in extra_pool if c != arrow_color]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:924:        arrow_color,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:932:        t[\"indicator_color\"] = arrow_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:934:    metrics = _combo_metrics(trains, arrow_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:43:    - B-only rooms no longer follow B's shown arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:65:      with three colors and a centroid-based arrow cue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:87:  - The A-only room anchors the normal meaning of a legend arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:88:  - The B-only room shows that B's displayed arrow is not the real motion cue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:109:      uses B's displayed arrow directly instead of sign(disp_A - disp_B).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:342:    two-legend rule instead of a one-color one-arrow lookup.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:353:    - A's true direction is the arrow shown inside A.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:354:    - B's true direction is not its own shown arrow; it is sign(A_display - B_display).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:361:    legend directions must itself be decoded from the relationship between the two visible arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/41/code.py:35:#    - The rule is intentionally narrowed to straight rays and single-gap generated samples. This keeps the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:17:      drawn as a coarse arrow silhouette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:18:    - The visible arrow direction is the legend's displayed vector.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:22:    - Suppose the left legend uses color 7 and shows a right arrow, so display_left = (0, 1).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:23:    - Suppose the right legend uses color 5 and shows a down arrow, so display_right = (1, 0).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:24:    - In the source task or in the pure arrow-shape variant, color-5 rooms would slide down.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:28:        2. decode each rectangle's arrow shape into a displayed vector;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:37:  - The arrow silhouettes are intentionally chunky and asymmetric, so humans can read them\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:38:    without needing centroid math, while the subtraction still creates a visible second-stage rule.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:55:    3. the dominant legend-color mask matches one of the 5x5/6x7 arrow templates;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:65:  - Humans can first read the left arrow in the usual way, then compare it to the right arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:66:  - Right-colored rooms visibly disobey their own displayed arrow and instead follow the vector\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:74:    original three-color centroid code.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:79:  - Color 1 is just a legend plate color; all nontrivial inference comes from arrow orientation\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:83:  - Metric: compare the combo output against a legacy-arrow output that incorrectly lets the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:84:    second wall color follow its own displayed arrow directly.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:90:  - main() assumes the dominant legend color is the wall color; generator uses masks where the arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:171:def _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr: int, dc: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:176:def _diagonal_arrow_mask(dr: int, dc: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:188:CARDINAL_TEMPLATE_MASKS = {(dr, dc): _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc) for dr, dc in CARDINAL_DIRS}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:189:DIAGONAL_TEMPLATE_MASKS = {(dr, dc): _diagonal_arrow_mask(dr, dc) for dr, dc in DIAGONAL_DIRS}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:194:    patch[_cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc)] = fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:200:    patch[_diagonal_arrow_mask(dr, dc)] = fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:210:def _match_arrow_direction(fill_mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:249:    direction = _match_arrow_direction(patch == fill_col)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:362:    Slide yellow pixels inside enclosed rooms using arrow legends plus a derived second instruction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:366:    - Two small legend rectangles sit on the top border. Each uses wall color C as a chunky arrow silhouette on a color-1 plate.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:370:    - Decode each legend's displayed direction from the orientation of its wall-colored arrow shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:375:    first read arrow shapes as vectors, then derive the second color's true motion by vector subtraction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/26/code.py:21:    - Extra pixels may enlarge a sprite's bbox, so the bbox center can disagree with the visually best symmetry axis.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/26/code.py:24:    - For each non-background color, compute its tight bounding box and take the bbox center as the vertical axis.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/6/code.py:63:#      one narrower opening so the contrast is present within each grid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/6/code.py:758:    narrow_candidates = [i for i, available in enumerate(available_per_block) if i != wide_idx and (1 in available or 2 in available)]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/6/code.py:759:    if not narrow_candidates:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/6/code.py:761:    narrow_idx = narrow_candidates[int(rng.integers(len(narrow_candidates)))]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/6/code.py:767:        elif i == narrow_idx:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/6/code.py:768:            narrow_sizes = [size for size in (1, 2) if size in available]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/6/code.py:769:            size = narrow_sizes[int(rng.integers(len(narrow_sizes)))]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/31/code.py:41:  `main()` must therefore compute scores exactly; a heuristic like \"pick the bbox center\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/6/code.py:46:#   direction relative to the coupler centroid is actually readable by the solver's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/16/code.py:24:#      original: two differently colored fenced rooms might use two different arrows, and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/31/code.py:64:  wide enough to be seen by eye, narrow enough that the frame grid still reads as one object.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/9/code.py:270:    - Read the sign of centroid(indicator) - centroid(center) to get one of 8 directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/8/code.py:34:#    strong enough to be visible: in every generated sprite, all noisy protrusions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/8/code.py:58:#    protrusions in the input all reappear on the opposite side in the output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/8/code.py:157:    - The task is still per-color symmetry reasoning, but the repair is not denoising by deletion. The one-sided protrusions identify the noisy side, and the output is obtained by moving those protrusions to the opposite side of the inferred axis.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:25:#    orthogonal cue, and the marker's distance to the coupler centroid is bucketed into\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:28:#    marker must attach above the body, and a near marker means its centroid position must lean\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:50:#    4-neighbor protrusion from the 8-body, never overlaps couplers or stripes, and is rejected\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:70:#    - Marker distance to the coupler centroid lands in one of two generator-controlled bins,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:238:def _centroid(coords):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:246:    cr, cc = _centroid(couplers)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:291:    br, bc = _centroid(body)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:323:      marker's placement around the 8-body supplies the orthogonal pose cue, and its distance to the coupler centroid chooses\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:523:                br, bc = _centroid(body_t)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:811:def _position_strength(marker, centroid, direction):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:812:    dr = marker[0] - centroid[0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:813:    dc = marker[1] - centroid[1]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/14/code.py:824:    body_ctr = _centroid(body)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/10/code.py:38:      \"legend color picks the chamber, legend arrow gives one of four directions, the yellow bar\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/10/code.py:339:    Slide yellow rigid line segments inside closed wall regions using cardinal legend arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/10/code.py:356:    - The legend color still selects which arrow applies to which chamber, but the yellow object is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/21/code.py:40:  - `_legend_directions(...)` assumed centroid offset should become a literal move vector. In the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:29:#    operation cannot do that: moving a lone protrusion across the axis preserves\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:35:#    Each sprite also receives at least one movable protrusion, so the flip\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:56:#    human can compare input/output and notice that protrusions jump across the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:61:#    the moved protrusions are easy to attribute to the correct object. No extra\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:68:#    Build a symmetric clean core first. Choose output-side protrusion cells that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:71:#    input-side protrusions. Oracle check requires `main(input) == output`.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:76:#    - Every generated protrusion has an empty mirrored destination, so the flip\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:156:    Flip each sprite's unmatched protrusions across its own strongest mirror axis.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:161:    - A few same-color pixels lack mirror partners and act as one-sided protrusions attached near the symmetric core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:223:# -- CFG: movable protrusions --\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:399:    \"\"\"Return output-side protrusion cells whose mirrored input-side cell is in-bounds and empty.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/3/code.py:432:    \"\"\"Add output-side protrusions to out and mirrored input-side protrusions to inp.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/5/code.py:13:# - The generator uses narrow funnel chambers so both changes are externally visible:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:20:# - The moving marker is no longer a 1-cell-thick line. It is a fixed 3x3 arrow sprite:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:25:# - The destination marker is an identical arrow sprite of the same color, translated further\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:26:#   along the arrow's pointing direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:27:# - To make \"direction from shape\" necessary, the arrow is attached only to a side orthogonal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:32:#   Variant logic: an arrow attached above may still point right, so the container moves right.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:33:#   The solver must read the arrow shape, then find the matching arrow destination along that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:37:# - The arrow has only four orientations and one fixed size, so the new degree of freedom is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:47:#   the subset of pixels closest to the border. With an arrow sprite, that \"closest layer only\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:48:#   extraction would lose most of the arrow and break matching.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:50:#   shape, matches it against four fixed arrow templates, and derives direction from that match.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:53:# - Each generated object reserves a rectangle covering: input container, input arrow, destination\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:54:#   arrow, moved container, and moved arrow. Reserved rectangles are packed with a 1-cell margin,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:56:# - The destination arrow is always at least one background cell away from the head arrow, which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:60:# - Because the arrow is orthogonally attached, the changed mechanism is visible in every mover:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:65:# - Only three object types remain on the board: container, source arrow, destination arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:66:#   The arrow is compact (3x3), monochrome, and separated from other assemblies by background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:70:#   cue that the viewer has to infer is the arrow orientation itself.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:114:def _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h: int, outer_w: int, attach_side: str, anchor: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:124:def _stamp_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, tl: tuple[int, int], direction: str, color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:141:    Move bordered containers toward same-colored arrow destinations, charging grey cells to orange.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:144:    - The source marker is a 3x3 arrow sprite, not a line.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:145:    - Direction is derived from the arrow's normalized shape, not from which side of the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:146:      container the arrow touches.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:147:    - The container and arrow translate toward the nearest matching arrow destination along\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:148:      the arrow direction, capped by the number of grey interior cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:342:        head_r, head_c = _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h, outer_w, attach_side, anchor)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:442:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (hr, hc), s[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:443:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (dr, dc), s[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:474:        _stamp_arrow(expected, (nhr, nhc), s[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/37/code.py:65:    class narrow while still preventing a brittle \"exactly one shape\" shortcut.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/34/code.py:70:    border legends are still border-touching solid rectangles with the same 3-color centroid code.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c416de3/18/code.py:27:#      square 0-ring and derive a unique bbox center from it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/36/code.py:89:    Direction is still decoded from centroid(indicator) - centroid(center).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/36/code.py:138:        center_centroid = np.mean(np.array(center, dtype=float), axis=0)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/36/code.py:139:        indicator_centroid = np.mean(np.array(indicator, dtype=float), axis=0)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/36/code.py:140:        vr, vc = (indicator_centroid - center_centroid).tolist()\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/36/code.py:230:    - Decode direction as sign(centroid(indicator) - centroid(center)), and decode the maximum travel distance from the number of indicator pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/13/code.py:378:    Launch wall-jammed yellow pixels using the legend arrows, with the touching wall causing the first rebound and later wall hits continuing the reflected path.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/13/code.py:390:    - The yellow pixels are not supposed to reverse-slide directly; they must be fired along the legend arrow from a wall-touching start, so the initial wall contact determines the rebound and the rest of the reflected route.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/17/code.py:34:   - Marker geometry is also narrow: a short 1xk or kx1 segment outside the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/48/code.py:207:    - Border legends are solid rectangles whose dominant fill color matches a wall color. Blue center pixels and red indicator pixels still encode direction by centroid offset, and the number of red pixels encodes the shared step count.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/48/code.py:268:    \"\"\"5x5 cardinal legend. Direction is the centroid offset; red-count is the fixed step count.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/33/code.py:36:#    The cascade is intentionally narrow: one ordered chain, one visible comparison key per step, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/23/code.py:14:#   where direction comes from centroid(indicator) - centroid(center).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/23/code.py:164:      two-color centroid comparison. It is encoded directly by the unique off-center pixel inside a compact\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:10:#     after: they are paired by the row/column rank signature of 8-connected hole-component centroids.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:20:#         under 8-connectivity they both collapse to 2 hole components whose centroids are TL and BR,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:28:#       Replace this with centroid-rank signatures.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:89:def _rank_signature(centroids: list[tuple[float, float]]) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], ...]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:90:    if not centroids:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:93:    rows = _compress_values([float(r) for r, _c in centroids])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:94:    cols = _compress_values([float(c) for _r, c in centroids])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:105:            for r, c in centroids\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:112:    centroids: list[tuple[float, float]] = []\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:117:        centroids.append((float(pts[:, 0].mean()), float(pts[:, 1].mean())))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:118:    return _rank_signature(centroids)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:231:      resulting hole-component centroids.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/40/code.py:232:    - Pair each frame with the sprite sharing that same centroid-rank arrangement, regardless of the diagonal slash\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/16/code.py:48:#    - A human can inspect an input/output pair and notice that some objects lose left/right protrusions while another\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/16/code.py:49:#      loses top-only or bottom-only protrusions. Because the critical sprite's noise spans both halves of the wrong\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:10:#    - D1/0 changes how direction is read: the legend is no longer a centroid puzzle; the wall\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:11:#      color itself forms a small arrow silhouette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:14:#    - Fusion rule used here: the arrow still has an orientation, but that orientation is not a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:16:#      the matching legend corner, the arrow tells us which of the two tangent directions to take\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:19:#    - Small trace: if a TL-corner legend contains a wall-colored arrow pointing east, then for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:22:#      step is east, it uses the counterclockwise track. The same arrow would have meant \"slide\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:28:#    - Arrow directions still come from a small discrete family (4 cardinals via 5x5 arrows, 4\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:29:#      diagonals via 6x7 arrows), avoiding excessive freedom while keeping the visual cue obvious.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:34:#    - Original `_legend_directions(...)` assumed three-color legends and centroid arithmetic.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:35:#      That is invalid now because legends are two-color arrow templates. The new solver matches\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:36:#      the dominant-color silhouette against 5x5 / 6x7 arrow masks.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:42:#    - Fusion-specific new assumption: the arrow direction must equal one of the two possible first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:47:#      so later-chosen 5x5 or 6x7 arrows never collide with walls.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:54:#      the legend visibly looks like an arrow rather than a dot-offset code, and the yellows stay\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:56:#    - Because the arrow matches the initial tangent at the anchor, samples make clear that the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:57:#      arrow is not a free-space motion vector: the yellows do not cut across the room.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:68:#    - The chosen arrow direction equals the clockwise first step or the counterclockwise first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:135:def _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr: int, dc: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:140:def _diagonal_arrow_mask(dr: int, dc: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:152:CARDINAL_TEMPLATE_MASKS = {(dr, dc): _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc) for dr, dc in CARDINAL_DIRS}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:153:DIAGONAL_TEMPLATE_MASKS = {(dr, dc): _diagonal_arrow_mask(dr, dc) for dr, dc in DIAGONAL_DIRS}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:164:        patch[_cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc)] = fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:167:    patch[_diagonal_arrow_mask(dr, dc)] = fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:207:def _match_arrow_direction(fill_mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:219:def _legend_arrow_info(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> dict[int, tuple[tuple[int, int], str]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:221:    Detect border legends and decode wall_color -> (arrow_direction, corner).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:224:    silhouette against arrow templates instead of comparing minority-color centroids.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:243:        direction = _match_arrow_direction(patch == fill)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:360:def _select_track_from_arrow(profile: dict[str, object], arrow_dir: tuple[int, int]) -> list[tuple[int, int]] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:363:    if arrow_dir == cw_dir and arrow_dir != ccw_dir:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:365:    if arrow_dir == ccw_dir and arrow_dir != cw_dir:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:368:    cw_score = _dir_score(cw_dir, arrow_dir)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:369:    ccw_score = _dir_score(ccw_dir, arrow_dir)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:394:    Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions along the inner wall, using arrow legends as entry tangents.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:400:      region wall color, and that dominant color is arranged as a visible arrow silhouette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:405:    - The legend arrow does not mean \"move straight through the room\". Instead it selects which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:410:    Key insight: The legend color still links the correct room, but the arrow now chooses the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:417:    legend_info = _legend_arrow_info(g, bg)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:449:        arrow_dir, corner = legend_info[border_col]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/41/code.py:453:        track = _select_track_from_arrow(profile, arrow_dir)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/6/code.py:123:    - indicator centroid right of center => clockwise, left => counterclockwise\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/19/code.py:63:#    exactly the same placement but prunes asymmetric protrusions according to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/14/code.py:33:      to a sign-only arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/14/code.py:47:  - `_legend_directions(...)` in the original solver assumed sign(centroid delta) was sufficient.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/14/code.py:48:    That would alias (+2,+1) with (+1,+1). The fused solver must preserve the full centroid delta\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/14/code.py:158:    The full center->indicator centroid delta is snapped to one of the 8 knight vectors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/21/code.py:40:#    - Object count is narrowed to exactly 4 (a 2x2 frame grid). This keeps the legend short and lets frame colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/4/code.py:51:    column), which keeps the visual language narrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:15:#   a 2-pixel arrow stub made from the transferable payload color,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:19:#   source.arrow -> dominant-axis direction to the target destination,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:32:#   in the encoded arrow direction. So arrow direction alone is insufficient.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:34:#   corners compatible with the chosen arrow direction. This keeps the symbolic alphabet small.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:42:#   that border class and then resolve the target by arrow direction plus marker quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:43:# - The D15-style directional cue is compressed to a 2-pixel center+neighbor arrow stub. That\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:46:#   3-pixel arrow while remaining a single readable object.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:51:# - Sources are always fully visible 3x3 boxes so both the arrow and the diagonal marker are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:53:# - Source placement is filtered so the two destinations are both in the source's arrow direction\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:58:# - The input visibly contains two deleted source boxes, each with an arrow and an extra corner\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:65:# - The arrow is a compact 2-pixel center+edge pointer, while the marker remains a single corner\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:75:# - For each real source, both destinations share the arrow direction but occupy the two marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:152:    assert patch[mr, mc] == border_color, \"marker corner must not overlap the arrow\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:409:def _detect_arrow_marker_sources(g: np.ndarray, bg: int, grey: int) -> list[dict]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:412:    The payload forms one of four 2-pixel arrow stubs and includes the center cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:413:    The marker is a singleton corner not occupied by the arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:523:    - Each real source box has three simultaneous parts: a shared border color, a 2-pixel payload-colored arrow stub, and one extra diagonal marker pixel in a corner.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:529:    - Among those same-border candidates, keep the unique source whose arrow points toward the destination and whose diagonal marker corner matches the destination's quadrant relative to that source.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:533:    - Matching is now layered: the destination center color selects a source border class, the arrow gives a coarse global direction, and the diagonal marker resolves which destination inside that direction cone is the true target.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:540:    sources = _detect_arrow_marker_sources(g, bg, grey)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:542:    assert sources, \"expected marked arrow sources\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:770:    sources = _detect_arrow_marker_sources(g, bg, GREY)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/28/code.py:84:    Clean each color-object by enforcing vertical symmetry around its bbox center.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/28/code.py:94:    Key insight: disconnected pieces of the same color must be merged before measuring symmetry; the decisive axis is the full sprite bbox center, not the axis that gives the highest match score.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:27:      by the frame side nearest to that component's centroid, so one sprite can receive several\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:117:      guarantee: candidate hole components are filtered by centroid-side uniqueness\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:198:def _centroid(cells: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> tuple[float, float]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:224:    values = sorted(_distance_map(int(h), int(w), _centroid(cells)).values())\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:240:            center = _centroid(shape)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:354:        side = _nearest_side_for_center(h, w, _centroid(cells))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:595:            side = _nearest_side_for_center(sprite_h, sprite_w, _centroid(cells))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8e5c0c38/D5/0/code.py:55:      are still just \"one-column red markers\", so the rule class stays narrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/22/code.py:191:    - Process foreground colors in priority order by compactness: clean the narrow reference color first by the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:12:#      1. Matching key becomes the row/column rank order of hole centroids.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:18:#         centroid arrangement. That forces the solver to extract component centroids rather than count cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:27:#      Replace it with a centroid-rank signature.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:51:#      show that exact shape is not the key; the transferable feature is the centroid layout.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:176:def _rank_signature(centroids: list[tuple[float, float]]) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], ...]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:177:    if not centroids:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:180:    row_vals = _compress_values([float(r) for r, _c in centroids])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:181:    col_vals = _compress_values([float(c) for _r, c in centroids])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:192:            for r, c in centroids\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:199:    centroids: list[tuple[float, float]] = []\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:204:        centroids.append((float(pts[:, 0].mean()), float(pts[:, 1].mean())))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:205:    return _rank_signature(centroids)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:257:      of their centroids: which holes are above/below and left/right of the others.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:258:    - Pair each frame with the sprite having the same centroid-rank signature, not merely the same number of holes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8e5c0c38/D1/0/code.py:62:#       inputs, and asymmetric protrusions are mirrored rather than pruned away.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8e5c0c38/D1/3/code.py:49:#    Across examples, the solver sees one-sided protrusions jump to the opposite\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8e5c0c38/D1/3/code.py:101:    - A few same-color pixels sit on only one side of that axis, acting as one-sided protrusions rather than matched pairs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/20270e3b/20/code.py:38:     * in this combo, if the base also has a non-connector protrusion at column 4 on some\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/20270e3b/20/code.py:39:       aligned row, the plug hits that protrusion first and stops with its nearest column at 3.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8e5c0c38/D1/4/code.py:33:#     - each sprite's true center is derived from its local bbox center plus placement offset, so solver search and generator labels agree\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/c7f57c3e/8/code.py:69:#     narrower rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/25/code.py:15:#      eligible component to its centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/25/code.py:18:#      local groups after centroid extraction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/25/code.py:27:#   centroid (4,4), only the triangle gets that offset shadow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35