from UD instead,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:42:#   Difference: quadrant position no longer selects the mirror; the arrow gives\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:46:# - The arrow vocabulary stays tiny: exactly two 3x3 glyphs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:49:#   arrows alone are insufficient, and anchors alone are ambiguous without first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:50:#   knowing which primary axis the arrow selected.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:60:#   must remove arrow markers before stamping or they would leak into the output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:62:#   arrows exist only outside TL, and the TL motif remains one 4-connected\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:66:# - The right arrow is drawn 1 cell above the anchor; the down arrow is drawn 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:71:# - The arrow is kept disconnected from the anchor, so component extraction for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:75:# - A human can read the arrow as the obvious first instruction, then notice\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:76:#   that two targets with the same arrow can still differ because of the anchor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:78:# - The generator enforces both arrow types, both anchor states, and at least\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:82:# - Green is reserved for arrows and never used inside the motif.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:84:#   anchor, while arrows sit outside with a one-cell gap. This avoids overlap and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:88:# - No hidden color ordering is used. Red still marks anchors, green means arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/cod