            # centroid: average over occupied cells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D2/3/code.py:304:    - Recolor each assembled piece by the legend patch cell reached by that piece's centroid in the final 2×2 layout.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D2/3/code.py:573:    # Compute centroid quadrant for each piece; all 4 must be distinct.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/23/code.py:337:    narrow_w = random.randint(1, n - 1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/23/code.py:338:    remove = n - narrow_w\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/25/code.py:35:#    - this fusion narrows the true transform family to morphology, introduces a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D8/1/code.py:217:    Recolor each piece by quantizing its centroid into a k×k grid, using legend_map.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D8/1/code.py:358:      legend-map cell reached by its centroid in the final rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/41/code.py:52:  - original blocker source: river narrows below width n,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:23:#    - D3/0: each meaningful source is now an arrow sprite, not an open frame\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:25:#    - D4/3: the ray starts at the arrow's touchpoint and continues to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:27:#    - D5/0: the output keeps the full canvas, so arrows remain visible outside\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:29:#    Example: a top-side arrow has its tip on row r0-1 at column c0+1+k and its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:30:#    head/shaft extend upward, so the arrow visibly points downward into the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:33:#    If the arrow uses two colors, the tip color is also the first ray color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:37:#    2-3 arrows per sample and commonly uses two-color arrows. To avoid visual\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:39:#    vertical rays (top/bottom arrows) or horizontal rays (left/right arrows).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:45:#      Meaningful arrows have exactly one ring-touching tip.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:51:#    The combo therefore needs a new primitive: detect one arrow tip, infer its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:56:#    for each ray, then places the matching arrow just outside the corresponding\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:63:#    - arrows visibly replace the old open rectangles\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:64:#    - each arrow points into the frame from the side where the ray starts\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:65:#    - the output keeps the whole scene, so the outside arrow and inside ray are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:72:#    outline, clearly separated arrows, and uncluttered interior stripes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:75:#    Monochrome arrows produce solid rays. Two-color arrows alternate along the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:87:#    - Meaningful components must have exactly one ring hit and a valid arrow-tip\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:93:#    - Distractors must not mimic an arrow touching the ring. The generator keeps\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:201:def _has_arrow_tip(coords: set[tuple[int, int]], tip: tuple[int, int], side: str) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:256:def _decode_arrow_components(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:287:        if side is None or not _has_arrow_tip(coords, tip, side):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:360:def _place_arrow_component(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:387:def _arrow_backbone(side: str, tip: tuple[int, int], length: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:487:    Reconstruct full-span interior rays from external arrows while preserving the full canvas.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:491:    - Useful external components are arrow sprites whose tips touch the ring just outside the frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:496:    - Reset the frame interior to its fill color, then decode each valid arrow tip as one boundary startpoint.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:498:    - Monochrome arrows produce solid rays; two-color arrows alternate starting from the visible tip color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:502:      \"the opposite side\" rather than \"another touchpoint\", solving reduces to detecting the arrow tip,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:515:    decoded = _decode_arrow_components(g, bg=bg, bc=bc, fill=fill, r0=r0, c0=c0, r1=r1, c1=c1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:624:        backbone = _arrow_backbone(side, tip, _ARROW_LEN)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:626:        if not _place_arrow_component(grid, comp_mask, backbone, head, colors, bg):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D8/2/code.py:20:#                  paint each piece from the palette cell containing its centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D8/2/code.py:281:    - Quantize piece centroids into the 2x2 assembled quadrant grid, then recolor every piece with the input color of the diagonally opposite quadrant's piece.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/6/code.py:346:    narrow_w = random.randint(1, n - 1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/6/code.py:347:    remove = n - narrow_w\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7b80bb43/8/code.py:28:#    The topology family is still narrow enough that 2-4 examples support the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:26:# - This variant adds explicit green arrow glyphs inside each non-TL quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:27:#   A right-pointing arrow means \"apply LR reflection\"; a down-pointing arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:28:#   means \"apply UD reflection\". If a quadrant shows both arrows, both flips are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:33:#   instead show a down arrow and therefore use only UD reflection; the copied motif\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:37:# - The symbol vocabulary is intentionally small: only two 3x3 arrow glyphs exist,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:48:#   anchors. With markers added, leaving `out` unchanged would leak arrows into the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:55:# - Marker arrows are placed relative to each target anchor with a one-cell gap:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:56:#   the LR arrow sits above the anchor, the UD arrow sits left of the anchor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:80:# - Each non-TL quadrant has one or two green arrow components and at least one arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:82:# - Each arrow glyph matches one of the two exact templates below.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:203:        assert grouped[key] >= 1, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain at least one arrow marker\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:210:    Copy the TL motif into the other quadrants, scaling by anchor size and mirroring by arrow markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:215:    - Each non-TL quadrant additionally contains one or two green arrow glyphs: a right arrow requests LR reflection, and a down arrow requests UD reflection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:219:    - For each target quadrant, read its arrow glyphs to decide which reflections to apply, then scale the transformed motif by the ratio between target-anchor size and TL-anchor size.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:222:    Key insight: The red anchor still tells you the scale, but mirror behavior is no longer implied by quadrant position; it is written explicitly by the nearby arrow symbols.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:443:def _paint_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, top: int, left: int, shape: frozenset[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:450:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r - 4, anchor_c, RIGHT_ARROW)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:452:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r, anchor_c - 4, DOWN_ARROW)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9bbf930d/D15/2/code.py:28:#    particle still sits on the border, but it now has one dedicated adjacent arrow-marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9bbf930d/D15/2/code.py:45:#    is rotated by 0/90/180/270 degrees and every start gets an explicit arrow marker. This\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9bbf930d/D15/2/code.py:52:#    - Treating only {BG, PARTICLE} as passable would make the arrow marker act like a wall.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9bbf930d/D15/2/code.py:66:#    Using one dedicated arrow color avoids overloading corridor colors. Markers only appear\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9bbf930d/D15/2/code.py:74:#    - Each movable particle is on the border and has exactly one 4-neighbor arrow marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9bbf930d/D15/2/code.py:75:#    - The arrow marker color appears only in these local start pairs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9bbf930d/D15/2/code.py:121:    \"\"\"Return border particles with a unique adjacent arrow marker.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/9bbf930d/D15/2/code.py:149:    \"\"\"Move particles through same-color corridors; initial direction comes from edge arrows.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1ae2feb7/3/code.py:292:        raise ValueError(\"bar side too narrow for sampled separator gap\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:19:#       1) use the arrow between the two training frames to read source -> destination,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:31:#         - read the arrow to fix direction,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:45:#     - The arrow only appears between horizontally or vertically adjacent training frames, which keeps the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:49:#       invalid once direction is explicitly marked, so the solver now decodes the arrow first.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:58:#       for a thin arrow to fit between any allowed training pair.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:59:#     - Sprite pixels live strictly inside frame interiors. The arrow is drawn only in the corridor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:62:#     - A human can see the arrow before reasoning about the transform.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:84:#     - `decode_training_direction()` assumes one isolated arrow component sits in the gap between the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:341:        arrow = _direction_from_component(pts, axis)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:342:        if arrow == \"right\":\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:344:        if arrow == \"left\":\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:373:    arrow = _direction_from_component(pts, axis)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:374:    if arrow == \"down\":\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:376:    if arrow == \"up\":\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:383:    Solve a framed sprite analogy where a directed arrow and three color roles specify a contour rule.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:389:    - A thin single-color arrow lies between the two training frames and points from the example source\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:393:    - Match the training pair by identical non-zero palettes, then decode the arrow to determine the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:401:    - The arrow removes direction ambiguity, while the color counts reveal the stable role.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:662:def draw_direction_arrow(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/15/code.py:813:    draw_direction_arrow(grid, frame_boxes[train_direction[0]], frame_boxes[train_direction[1]], marker_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:27:#    - The bar changes from a 7-wide base with 4 evenly spaced protrusions to a 9-wide base.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:28:#    - Four \"matching\" protrusions are fixed at local cols 1,3,5,7 so that, after the correct shift,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:30:#    - One or two extra protrusions are added at local col 0 and/or 8, giving 5-6 protrusions total.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:31:#      These extras are visible in the input, but only the central 4-protrusion core is inserted into\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:32:#      the frame. The unmatched edge protrusions disappear instead of moving with the useful core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:34:#      old task: 4 source protrusion cols -> translate so all 4 align with the 4 colored columns.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:35:#      new task: 5-6 source protrusion cols -> choose the translation whose shifted protrusion set covers\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:41:#      but it avoids combinatorial ambiguity from arbitrary extra protrusion placement.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:43:#      extra idea: ignore unmatched protrusions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:46:#      can have more protrusions than destination columns.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:47:#    - Any logic that assumes the whole bar moves as one piece is now wrong; unmatched protrusions must\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:52:#    - The right extra protrusion is constrained to height 2, so it reaches only row 4. This avoids\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:56:#    - The extra protrusions are visible at the left and/or right edge of the inserted bar.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:57:#    - Human solvers can see that colors climb only above the 4 protrusions that line up with colored\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:64:#      vertically above matched protrusions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:67:#    - There are always 5 or 6 visible protrusions in the bar.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:68:#    - The 4 active protrusions all have height >= 2, so they are detectable as protrusions above\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:71:#    - The best horizontal shift is the one with maximum overlap between shifted protrusion cols and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:74:#      matched protrusions, and the generator guarantees that span is the intended central core.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:76:#    - Every colored slot column has a matched protrusion after the chosen translation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:81:    Slide a wider grey bar into the frame, but only the protrusions aligned with colored columns matter.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:85:    - The frame still contains exactly four colored columns, while the bar now has five or six upward protrusions on a wider base.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:89:    - Choose the horizontal shift that makes the largest subset of protrusions coincide with the four colored columns.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:90:    - Insert only the minimal contiguous core covering those matched protrusions, then re-stack each column's colors immediately above its matched protrusion.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D8/2/code.py:93:    - The extra protrusions are distractors. The correct move is to isolate the four protrusions that actually match the four target columns and discard the unmatched edge teeth.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/30/code.py:32:     real chokepoint narrower than n and the mover always halts upstream.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/30/code.py:75:  human to infer \"the river gets erased behind the sliding block until the first too-narrow gate.\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/30/code.py:383:    narrow_w = random.randint(1, n - 1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/30/code.py:384:    remove = n - narrow_w\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D1/1/code.py:27:#     - Old logic could color the 29-cell piece with 1 if its centroid lands in the lower-left quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D1/1/code.py:33:#     - To avoid silent regression to the original behavior, generated samples reject the identity case where area-rank order exactly matches centroid-quadrant order.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D1/1/code.py:37:#     - Exact-cover search still depends on shape geometry, but the new matching signal no longer uses centroid position.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D1/1/code.py:38:#     - The previous centroid-to-quadrant recolor block is invalid under this variant and must be replaced by area sorting.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D1/1/code.py:535:def _centroid_quadrant(mask: np.ndarray, side: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D1/1/code.py:570:    qcells = [_centroid_quadrant(mask, side) for mask in masks]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/58490d8a/D5/0/code.py:64:#     - the narrower legend only reduces occupied area; it does not introduce new collisions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/24/code.py:23:#    with a one-cell border ring, and each connector is a two-pixel red protrusion on that ring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/24/code.py:36:#    2-6, and each connector is always a two-pixel side protrusion. Symmetry is fixed to one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/24/code.py:46:#      red protrusions only in the one-cell border ring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/24/code.py:153:    assert seen == reds, \"red pixels must be exactly two-pixel side protrusions\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:14:#      then both training frames and both test/blank frames have right-edge gaps, and a thin arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:19:#      corridor arrow -> transform the test sprite -> write it into the blank frame -> return the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:27:#    - The arrow and the frame gaps encode the same direction redundantly. That redundancy is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:34:#      both the gap side and the arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:40:#    - Frames are placed on a 2x2 structured layout with minimum corridor width, so arrows fit\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:43:#      erased -> interior sprite pasted -> corridor arrows drawn. This keeps each layer separable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:45:#    - A human sees four obviously broken frames, two arrows, and one empty destination frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:54:#      original rule families, while direction is conveyed structurally by gap orientation and arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:481:def decode_arrow_direction(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:576:    arrow_dir = decode_arrow_direction(grid, frames, pair)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:577:    if gap_dir is None or arrow_dir is None or gap_dir != arrow_dir:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:602:    - The training pair and the test/blank pair each have a thin arrow in the corridor between them,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:603:      and the arrow direction matches the shared gap side of that pair.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:607:      the source->destination order from the combined gap-side cue and corridor arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:1026:def draw_direction_arrow(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:1169:    draw_direction_arrow(grid, frame_boxes[train_dir[0]], frame_boxes[train_dir[1]], marker_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/20/code.py:1170:    draw_direction_arrow(grid, frame_boxes[test_blank_dir[0]], frame_boxes[test_blank_dir[1]], marker_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/14/code.py:400:    use_narrow_hint = (n == 1 and random.random() < 0.75) or (n >= 2 and not reached)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/14/code.py:489:    if use_narrow_hint:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/14/code.py:523:        if use_narrow_hint and not geo_ok:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/14/code.py:525:        if not use_narrow_hint and geo_ok != dest_ok:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D1/3/code.py:45:#     - 原 main() 依赖“颜色只用于识别 legend，piece 的最终颜色来自空间位置”。这个前提已失效，必须移除 centroid 着色。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D1/3/code.py:53:#     - 连接器像素直接涂在 piece 已占据的边界单元上，不引入额外 protrusion，因此不会破坏 exact-cover 面积。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/12/code.py:12:#   or at the farthest reachable interior placement before a narrow gate.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/12/code.py:51:#   examples, it stops inside just before a narrow section. No overlapping markers or\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/12/code.py:296:    narrow_w = random.randint(1, n - 1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/12/code.py:297:    remove = n - narrow_w\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/4c3d4a41/D1/3/code.py:61:#     - Humans can see the difference by comparing which tall/short protrusion ends up\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D16/0/code.py:23:#      - Old rule after assembly: piece color = legend cell from centroid quantized into a 2x2 box.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D16/0/code.py:26:#      - Difference: the top triangle can have a centroid in the old top-left or top-right box, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D16/0/code.py:35:#      - The old centroid->quadrant code depended on rectangular quadrants; that assumption is removed.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D16/0/code.py:46:#    axis-aligned centroid rule would produce the same coloring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D16/0/code.py:332:    contains the centroid\". Instead, each color belongs to one diagonal side-sector of the final square.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:18:#   - D11/0 removes direction guessing. A thin arrow is drawn in the corridor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:30:#       3. decode the arrow so direction is fixed before rule inference\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:44:#     after adding a dedicated anchor color, an arrow color, a frame color, and a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:51:#   - find_square_frames() still works because the arrow never touches a frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:57:#     explicit and should be decoded from the arrow instead of guessed.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:62:#     * the training pair is axis-adjacent with a unique arrow component in its gap\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:68:#     and column gap, so the arrow fits cleanly between adjacent training frames.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:69:#   - The arrow is drawn only in the corridor between the chosen pair and never\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:78:#   - The arrow is a separate unique-color component outside all frames, so it does\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:90:#     repeats the same joint pattern: arrow fixes direction, anchor stays fixed,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:333:        arrow = _direction_from_component(pts, axis)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:334:        if arrow == \"right\":\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:336:        if arrow == \"left\":\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:365:    arrow = _direction_from_component(pts, axis)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:366:    if arrow == \"down\":\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:368:    if arrow == \"up\":\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:375:    Learn an anchor-aware contour rule from a framed training pair with an explicit arrow, then solve the test sprite.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:380:    - Every non-blank sprite contains exactly one shared-color anchor pixel, and a thin arrow outside the frames points from the training source frame to the training destination frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:384:    - Use the arrow to decide the training direction, then verify that the destination equals the 4-connected outer boundary of the source body, where the body means all non-zero pixels except the anchor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:388:    - The anchor is not ordinary foreground. It stays visible but should be mentally removed before tracing the body's outline, and the arrow removes any need to guess which training frame is the source.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:604:def draw_direction_arrow(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/9/code.py:695:    draw_direction_arrow(grid, frame_boxes[train_direction[0]], frame_boxes[train_direction[1]], marker_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D3/1/code.py:46:#     - recolor by assembled centroid quadrant, then paint in fixed layer order\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D3/1/code.py:87:#   - each layer keeps one output color, chosen from the legend by its assembled centroid quadrant\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D3/1/code.py:94:#   - centroid quantization of each anchored layer still lands in its home quadrant\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D3/1/code.py:247:    - Recolor each anchored sprite by the legend color of its assembled 2x2 centroid cell, then paint the layers in fixed reading order so later layers overwrite earlier ones.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/18/code.py:349:    - Each activated blob launches a ray from its uniquely narrowest extreme side, with width 1 or 2 from that side.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/37/code.py:552:    - The final assembly is valid only if every original red connector is paired exactly once and every reconstructed internal hole is occupied by a neighboring sprite's protrusion.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D13/3/code.py:19:#      protrusion. The TL source anchor points to the patch's bottom-right\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D13/3/code.py:21:#      reflection, the protrusion points to a different corner in each target\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D13/3/code.py:63:#      centroid lies in the same corner direction as the hook for every non-TL\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D13/3/code.py:106:    - Each quadrant contains exactly one red connected anchor; the anchor is a hooked shape with a visible corner-facing protrusion.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:17:#   Example trace: a piece whose assembled centroid lands in the top-left quadrant used to receive\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:20:#   The generator must preserve four distinct palette colors and four distinct centroid quadrants.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:26:#   and centroid-to-quadrant quantization all remain valid. The only invalid old assumption was the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:51:#   embeds exactly one interior 2x2 palette, and preserves one piece per centroid quadrant, so this\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:203:    Recolor each piece by quantizing its centroid into a k×k grid, then reading colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:299:        # Determine legend-map cell for each piece by centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:303:            # centroid: average over occupied cells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:337:    - Use each piece centroid to determine which 2×2 quadrant of the assembled square it occupies.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/1/code.py:589:    # Compute centroid quadrant for each piece; all 4 must be distinct.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/3/code.py:14:#    - After assembly, a piece is still assigned to one (row, col) quadrant by its centroid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/3/code.py:226:    Recolor each piece by quantizing its centroid into a k x k grid.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D4/3/code.py:356:    - Use each piece's centroid to decide which row and column of the assembled k x k layout it belongs to.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/31/code.py:164:        raise AssertionError(\"vertical cross must have one uniquely narrow end arm\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/31/code.py:183:    raise AssertionError(\"horizontal cross must have one uniquely narrow end arm\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/31/code.py:188:    Build a red ray network by activating single-axis cross blobs whose narrowest arm is a hidden port.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/31/code.py:196:    - The activated cross then emits a ray from the uniquely narrowest arm tip, with width 1 or 2 depending on that arm.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/31/code.py:199:    Key insight: Direction is encoded geometrically, not by color. The only arm end that is strictly narrower than the other three extremes is the implicit launch port.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5961cc34/31/code.py:334:# - a constant narrow top arm (width 1 or 2),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:25:#    - The useful outer component is now a bent arrow, not an open rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:27:#    - The arrow's pointing side is read from the tip orientation:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:28:#         top-ring tip  -> arrow points down\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:29:#         bottom-ring   -> arrow points up\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:30:#         left-ring     -> arrow points right\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:31:#         right-ring    -> arrow points left\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:35:#    - Suppose the frame interior is 6x9 and an arrow tip touches the top ring at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:38:#    - In this combo, the same tip maps to interior start (0,3), but the arrow is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:41:#    - If the arrow uses two colors, the tip color is still the first color and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:49:#    - vertical family: top/bottom arrows create full columns\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:50:#    - horizontal family: left/right arrows create full rows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:58:#      endpoint is now implicit: it is always the far boundary in the arrow's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:62:#      components are one-arrow-per-ray with a single startpoint.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:71:#    the cropped interior will be painted, then constructs corner-local bent arrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:72:#    outside the frame whose tips project to those starts. Each arrow:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:77:#    - uses a corner-local outer bend so different arrows stay separated\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:82:#    - the external cues are arrows rather than open rectangular frames\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:84:#    The repeated presence of both top and bottom arrows (or left and right arrows)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:90:#    the frame. The outer arrows remain parked in separate corner bands around the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:91:#    frame, so the frame border, startpoints, and arrowheads stay readable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:94:#    Color behavior remains structural rather than numeric. Monochrome arrows yield\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:95:#    solid rays. Two-color arrows alternate starting from the tip color, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:96:#    only color-order cue needed by the solver is directly visible on the arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:97:#    This also makes direction matter: a top arrow and a bottom arrow on different\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:103:#    - original a6 expects two ring touches and therefore misses the arrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:104:#    - D3-only would decode the same bent arrow into an adjacent-side L endpoint,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:106:#    The generator therefore always builds bent arrows whose adjacent-side tail\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:223:def _has_arrow_tip(coords: set[tuple[int, int]], tip: tuple[int, int], side: str) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:282:    Rebuild full-span interior rays from external bent arrows around a framed rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:286:    - Useful outer components are bent arrows with exactly one ring-touching tip on the frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:292:    - For each valid arrow, map its tip to the corresponding interior boundary cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:293:    - Starting from that cell, extend a straight row or column in the arrow's direction until the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:295:    - Monochrome arrows paint a solid ray; two-color arrows alternate along the ray beginning with\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:300:      arrow direction determines both the ray orientation and which far side acts as the stop.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:341:        if not _has_arrow_tip(coords, tip, start_side):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:415:def _place_arrow_component(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:473:def _arrow_specs(kind: str, start_side: str, H: int, W: int, r0: int, c0: int, r1: int, c1: int):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:647:        inner, backbone, head = _arrow_specs(kind, start_side, H, W, r0, c0, r1, c1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:663:        if not _place_arrow_component(grid, comp_mask, backbone, head, colors, bg):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D15/0/code.py:25:#   Everything before that (legend detection, palette extraction, exact cover, centroid-to-palette assignment)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D15/0/code.py:194:    Recolor each piece by quantizing its centroid into a k×k grid, using legend_map.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/f560132c/D15/0/code.py:320:    - Recolor each assembled piece from the palette cell selected by its centroid position in the 2x2 layout.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4c7dc4dd/13/code.py:40:#    The rule family is narrowed to symmetry transforms because that is the most\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/17/code.py:32:#     - tabs are narrower than the strip, which makes concave/convex shoulders visible without exploding the 