ly the minimal rectangle covering visited ray cells. The arrow and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:32:# - Using a single arrow could be too easy if the path were short, so the generator\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:37:# - The generator keeps wall shapes to single pixels. This narrows one degree of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:44:# - The original solver allowed many arrows. That logic still works, but the combo\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:45:#   generator now guarantees exactly one arrow. Solver-side arrow detection by\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:48:# - Wall detection as \"all non-bg cells not in arrows\" still holds, but the generator\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:49:#   must now prevent accidental wall components from mimicking arrow-shaped 3-cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:62:# - Every ray cell must stay inside bounds, avoid the arrow cells, avoid all previous\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:71:# - In examples, the input shows one small arrow near the upper-left plus many detached\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:88:# - To preserve arrow detectability, wall colors are assigned greedily so orthogonally\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:96:# - Assumption: there is exactly one arrow in combo samples. Guaranteed by generating\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:98:#   accidental second arrow component.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:105:def _is_arrow_pts(pts: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/code.py:113:def _arrow_dir_and_start(pts: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], tuple[int, int]] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/142ca369/17/cod