 Detect border legends and decode direction from the dominant-color arrow silhouette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/0/code.py:173:    of C-cells inside the rectangle against a small set of arrow templates.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/0/code.py:194:        direction = _match_arrow_direction(fill_mask)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/0/code.py:240:      border color. The C-cells form an arrow silhouette that directly indicates direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/0/code.py:245:    - The orientation of the C-colored arrow inside that legend gives the slide direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/0/code.py:249:    shape of the dominant-color cells, not the centroid offset of two minority colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a25697e4/28/code.py:390:      generated tasks connector color already narrows the candidate set, and the final validation\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:22:#   centroid, measure its Manhattan distance to the board center, reduce that distance\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:29:#   centroid is Manhattan distance 7 from the board center; then `(-7) % 4 == 1`.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:36:#   centroids are either integer/integer or half/half. That keeps centroid Manhattan\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:46:#   The new logic additionally assumes the cue centroid / board-center Manhattan distance\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:53:#   a target centroid distance residue. The generator therefore enumerates candidate cue\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:147:def _coords_centroid(coords: np.ndarray) -> tuple[float, float]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:154:    assert abs(dist - dist_i) < 1e-6, \"expected an integral centroid-to-center Manhattan distance\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:171:    - Rotate that color layer by a number of quarter-turns determined by the cue centroid's Manhattan distance to the board center, taken mod 4.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:203:        cue_center = _coords_centroid(outside_comps[0][\"coords\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:398:def _patch_centroid_offset(patch: np.ndarray) -> tuple[float, float]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:400:    return _coords_centroid(coords)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:416:    \"\"\"Place a cue patch on the canvas, matching one of the requested centroid distances.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D2/2/code.py:420:    off_r, off_c = _patch_centroid_offset(patch)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D15/2/code.py:117:    Infer direction from the single-pixel protrusion and return the true ring bbox.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/3/code.py:8:#    - 原题里图例只编码方向。现在图例仍然用 `sign(centroid(red) - centroid(center))` 编码方向，\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/3/code.py:95:      sign(centroid(indicator) - centroid(center))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/3/code.py:230:    The center->indicator centroid offset gives direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D5/0/code.py:25:#    The change is kept narrow: the wall parsing, seed selection, interior flood-fill and obstacle shadows are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d8e07eb2/20/code.py:165:    The answer is not found by global glyph identity. Color only narrows the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D6/1/code.py:55:#     narrowed to same-color-with-frame components only.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/2/code.py:17:#        by counts/centroids, then direction = sign(indicator centroid - center centroid).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/2/code.py:21:#      - Difference: direction is no longer read from a 2D centroid offset inside a solid\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/2/code.py:34:#      - direction comes from their centroids\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/2/code.py:36:#      remain valid. Rectangle, centroid, and centrality assumptions are false, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D1/2/code.py:452:    rectangle-centroid parser, while any sample containing an active diagonal L legend cannot.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/37/code.py:31:#    period (AABB). This keeps the rule rich enough to be seen but narrow enough\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D16/2/code.py:64:  - The bottleneck is visible as a narrow 1-cell neck between two larger rooms.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D16/2/code.py:65:  - At least one yellow is placed so its arrow points through that neck. Under the variant it\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D10/0/code.py:69:    frame is the unique nearest same-color frame under L1 distance between bbox centers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D10/0/code.py:121:    \"\"\"Return doubled bbox center coordinates to avoid floats.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D19/1/code.py:323:    Key insight: Wall color is no longer decorative; it selects the geometric parameter of the outward radiation, so the same wall shape produces a narrower beam when its border is red.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/18/code.py:23:#   Walls isolate one narrow chamber, so a human can see the front maroon disappear first,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/18/code.py:293:    - Magenta walls form narrow corridors near the destination.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D1/3/code.py:27:#     - each board layer is generated with a distinct mass centroid\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D1/3/code.py:33:#     - suppose outer/mid/inner input-layer centroids are at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D1/3/code.py:43:#   - To avoid ambiguity, generator enforces centroid separation and places cues\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D1/3/code.py:51:#   - The original generator could let all layer centroids nearly coincide\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D1/3/code.py:54:#     resulting input-layer centroids before placing cues.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/6ffbe589/D1/3/code.py:232:    # --- STEP 2) Match outside cues to layer centroids by row/column alignment ---\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D3/0/code.py:106:    The sign of centroid(indicator) - centroid(center) gives the legend direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/32/code.py:28:#      k-th outward depth layer remains visible, where k is 2 for narrow/medium\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D3/1/code.py:42:  - `_legend_directions(...)` assumed centroid offset should become a literal `(dr, dc)` move.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D3/1/code.py:184:    - indicator centroid right of center => clockwise, left => counterclockwise\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/a47bf94d/D10/0/code.py:19:# - Complexity calibration: restricting to a single cycle could become too narrow if the cycle order\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/17/code.py:72:    Key insight: The endpoints no longer define immediate full-width radiation; they identify a narrow outlet, and the exterior fill must respect a fixed bottleneck width first, then a gradually widening cone.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b9e38dc0/D8/2/code.py:25:#     - The change is deliberately narrow: only one new visible structure is introduced, and it is repeated twice per sample\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:20:# - The head is no longer a full edge line. It is a fixed 3x3 arrow sprite:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:26:#   in the arrow's pointing direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:27:# - The translation distance is measured from the arrow tip to the point, not from arrow bbox\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:28:#   top-left and not from a full line center. After moving k steps, the arrow tip advances by k and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:33:#   D4/2 alone: an arrow attached on the left may still point down, so direction comes from shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:35:#   Combo here: an arrow attached orthogonally to the motion axis points toward a single dot, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:36:#   the arrow tip rather than any bbox anchor is what aligns to that dot.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:37:#   Example: a right-pointing arrow attached above the container has tip at (r, c). A point sits at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:42:# - The arrow vocabulary is still only four shapes, keeping shape parsing low-entropy.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:43:# - To ensure this is visibly different from the source task, the generator only attaches arrows on\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:46:# - The target is always a point with at least one background cell between it and the arrow tip, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:51:#   Broken. In the combo, the arrow may be above the box while motion is right.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:55:#   top-left. Broken again: a point has no meaningful shared shape anchor with the arrow. The new\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:56:#   anchor is the arrow tip.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:58:#   between arrow tip and point. The solver still contains a tiny fallback for malformed gap=0\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:59:#   inputs by trying to peel one extra pixel off an otherwise valid arrow component.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:62:# - Each generated assembly reserves the union of five regions: input container, input arrow,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:63:#   destination point, moved container, moved arrow. Packing is done with a 1-cell background moat.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:68:# - Across samples, the arrow is visibly not on the side of travel, forcing the solver to read its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:73:# - Only three foreground object types appear: bordered container, arrow head, point target.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:74:#   The point is sparse, the arrow is compact, and assemblies are separated by background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:78:#   direction, so the only new inference burden is the fused geometry: arrow orientation + tip to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:82:# - Head components are exact 3x3 arrow sprites, or that sprite plus one fused forward point in the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:84:# - Every mover has exactly one nearest forward point on its arrow ray; the generator verifies\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:86:# - Motion direction, fill order, and point alignment are all keyed off the arrow direction, not\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:147:def _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h: int, outer_w: int, attach_side: str, anchor: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:157:def _stamp_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, tl: tuple[int, int], direction: str, color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:180:def _parse_arrow_component(pts: set[tuple[int, int]]) -> dict | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:226:    - The source marker is a 3x3 arrow sprite whose orientation gives the movement direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:228:    - Distance is measured from the arrow tip to the target point.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:290:        parsed = _parse_arrow_component(set(comp0[\"pts\"]))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:422:        head_r, head_c = _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h, outer_w, attach_side, anchor)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:531:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (hr, hc), s[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:563:        _stamp_arrow(expected, (nhr, nhc), s[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D3/3/code.py:20:#       direction: sign(centroid(indicator) - centroid(center)) still provides the initial movement vector\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:36:#             and carve a virtual breach through the capped side selected by the arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:43:#        and places the outside arrow one gap away from the mouth side.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:49:#      separate from the outside arrow and from sparse obstacles.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:50:#    - The outside arrow has only four possible directions and always uses one 2-pixel\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:69:#      obstacle. That would incorrectly make the external arrow cast shadows, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:73:#      arrow first and then score the trigger's 4-neighbors by dot product with that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:74:#      arrow direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:93:#      for where the exterior radiation should go is the detached 2-pixel arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:94:#    - Across examples the same wall family appears with different arrow directions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:99:#    - The two markers live in different zones: trigger on the wall, arrow outside\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:102:#      that could be mistaken for the outside arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:118:#      distances from its two pixels; generator makes that the only external arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:121:#    - The trigger has at least one passable 4-neighbor in the arrow direction and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:332:    - Treat the trigger as part of the wall, decode the arrow to decide which capped side becomes a virtual opening, and start flood-fill from the trigger's inner-side neighbor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:335:    Key insight: Neither old cue survives on its own: the fill source is no longer inside the enclosure, and the opening is no longer visible in the wall shape, so the solver must combine the border trigger with the detached arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:436:    assert best_score > 0, \"expected trigger interior to lie in the arrow-selected direction\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:635:    dist_arrow = distance_transform_cdt(~dir_marker_mask, metric=\"chessboard\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:636:    eligible = (grid == bg) & (dist_solid > 1) & (dist_arrow > 1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/38/code.py:381:    - An outer-arc gap emits a narrow band toward the right side of the grid, while a bridge gap emits a vertical band toward the nearest border; each band is bounded by rays launched from the two wall tips around that gap.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D4/0/code.py:55:    color it controls. The direction is still sign(centroid(indicator) - centroid(center)).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D4/0/code.py:243:    \"\"\"5x5 cardinal legend. Direction is the centroid offset; red-count is the step count.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/221dfab4/D12/2/code.py:41:#    steps, and unless at least two decoded green stripes visibly flood pixels outside the narrow span. This makes the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/33/code.py:248:    - A unique red cell marks the middle of a green 3x3 destination block, while maroon pixels sit on narrow floor corridors or in isolated pockets outside it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/88e364bc/D4/2/code.py:40:#    The variant is intentionally narrow: legends, colors, wall generation, and region detection stay unchanged.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:21:# - Outside the container there is now a 3x3 maroon arrow head plus a matching\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:22:#   3x3 maroon destination arrow further along the travel direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:25:#     2. Use the external arrow only as the movable head sprite and as a visual\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:27:# - To make that visible, the head arrow is always attached on a side orthogonal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:30:#     * internal orange cells form a right-pointing arrow template\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:31:#     * external arrow head also points right, but it is attached above or below\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:34:#       destination arrow to the right of the source arrow, then move container\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:38:#     * fused: internal orange arrow decides direction, head position is decoupled\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:39:#       from direction, and the head itself is a compact arrow sprite\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:43:# - The internal cue is a fixed 4-cell arrow template, not an arbitrary shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:44:# - The external cue is a fixed 3x3 arrow sprite.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:53:#   still keep its identity. Broken: a 3x3 arrow must be tracked as a full shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:55:#   must depend on the internal arrow direction instead.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:56:# - New assumption introduced here: movers have a unique internal arrow template.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:60:# - New assumption introduced here: the external arrow direction agrees with the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:61:#   internal arrow direction. Generator guarantees this and solver checks it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:65:#     input container, input head arrow, destination arrow,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:66:#     moved container, moved head arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:70:#   arrows from appearing between a source head and its own destination.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:76:# - The head arrow points the same way as the internal motif, acting as a second\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:81:# - Each assembly contains one container, one source arrow, one destination arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:83:#   head lands exactly on its destination arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:87:# - Orange growth is structurally tied to the same inferred direction: the arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:92:#   old same-side head as an arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:131:def _arrow_template(inner_h: int, inner_w: int, direction: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:156:    base = _arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:185:        template = set(_arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:193:def _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h: int, outer_w: int, attach_side: str, anchor: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:203:def _stamp_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, tl: tuple[int, int], direction: str, color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:233:    - Read direction from the internal orange arrow template.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:234:    - The adjacent external 3x3 arrow is the movable head sprite, but its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:236:    - Move container + head toward the nearest matching arrow destination along\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:370:    for r, c in _arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:414:        head_r, head_c = _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h, outer_w, attach_side, anchor)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:516:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (hr, hc), spec[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:517:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (dr, dc), spec[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:551:        _stamp_arrow(expected, (nhr, nhc), spec[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/hom