se\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/8/code.py:354:        raise ValueError(\"grid too narrow for dependency corridor\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/1/code.py:544:    \"\"\"Carve a narrow bg separator from the left edge without disconnecting path.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/de809cff/D10/0/code.py:99:    Remove one-cell specks/protrusions before reading dent geometry.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/de809cff/D10/0/code.py:201:      isolated specks fall back to black, and one-cell protrusions revert to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/dd6b8c4b/D7/1/code.py:47:#    Trails occupy narrow corridors outside the destination block and do not overlap the destination or\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:79:#   small 5-pixel arrow in a dedicated marker color; its center cell is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:90:#   may exist in multiple directions around the new head; only the arrow on the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:97:#   still recognizably the same family. The only new object type is the arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:104:#   frame. That no longer holds because marker arrows have their own color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:122:# - The rule is human-visible: markers look like tiny arrows, and the sprite’s\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:123:#   final orientation can be set by the last arrow even when no further target\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:287:    - Each frame contains one actor-colored triangular sprite plus marker-colored mini-arrows whose center cells are future landing points.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:291:    - Repeatedly move the head to the nearest marker center on that facing half-line, consume the whole marker glyph, and then rotate the sprite to the consumed marker's arrow direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D3/2/code.py:294:    Key insight: The sprite's current facing chooses which half-line to search, while each consumed marker dictates the next facing, so the arrows act as turn instructions rather than passive dots.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/17/code.py:21:#   - Width is restricted to the small visible set {2, 3}; that is narrow enough\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/17/code.py:33:#     non-background population. The generator keeps the narrow band dominant over\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/17/code.py:481:    \"\"\"Stable 15x15 narrow-band layout with a readable left-edge separator.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D17/0/code.py:28:#    The custom template family is still arrow/triangle-like so the task stays\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/5545f144/D17/0/code.py:49:#    The legend is always a narrow left panel with a lone centered sprite and no\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b5ca7ac4/D17/0/code.py:62:#    - The output still uses two narrow, well-separated side bands. There is no central clutter and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/10/code.py:19:# - Fused task: the source is now a 3-cell arrowhead whose geometry fixes the first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/10/code.py:24:#   is read as [red, blue, yellow]. An up-pointing yellow arrow starts at yellow,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/10/code.py:28:# - Markers use only four unambiguous arrowhead shapes `> < ^ v`; this keeps the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/10/code.py:42:#   every decoded arrow tip must point to an ambient path cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/10/code.py:46:#   arrows remain separate 8-connected components.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/195c6913/10/code.py:52:# - Humans can see two independent cues in the input: arrow orientation decides the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ces